#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186096843 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186096402 :question/id #uuid "2c37ae73-233d-5e0d-87ff-05cacc6d078b" :question/math-academy-id "e-183" :question/problem "Given that $f(x) = \\dfrac{1}{\\sqrt{1-3x}},$ calculate $f'(x).$" :question/worked-solution "Let $g(x)=1-3x$ and $f(g(x)) = \\dfrac{1}{\\sqrt{g}} = g^{-1/2}.$ \n\nDifferentiating, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(g(x)) & =({g}^{-1/2}{)}^{′}=-\\frac{1}{2}{g}^{-3/2}=-\\frac{1}{2{g}^{3/2}}, \\\\ {g}^{′}(x) & =(1-3x{)}^{′}=-3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, using the chain rule, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & ={f}^{′}(g(x))\\cdot {g}^{′}(x) \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{2{g}^{3/2}}\\cdot (-3) \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{2{g}^{3/2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{2(1-3x{)}^{3/2}}\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "0a312840-0d35-50bb-94b3-b04778f4efc3" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186096972 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "bd726c27-0178-5cc8-a8b6-82e60d62ac1b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3(15x^{4} - 8x)(3x^{5} - 4x^{2})^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186093807} {:answer/id #uuid "c477f2d6-5ad8-52b3-8fe5-92a70939ea8b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3(5x^{5} - 8x)(3x^{5} - 4x^{2})^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186093808} {:answer/id #uuid "060329dc-443f-5ee6-a297-ff049fb57b80" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-8(15x^{4} - 8x)(3x^{5} - 4x^{2})^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186093809} {:answer/id #uuid "113fc8bd-4460-5e48-a12a-43e5a61ddd92" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3(15x^{4} - 8x)(3x^{5} - 4x^{2})^{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186093810} {:answer/id #uuid "5927f4b1-57be-52eb-ac32-2223134b64df" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3(5x^{4} - 8x)(3x^{5} - 4x^{2})^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186093811}] :answer-field/id #uuid "9edef6e7-9845-5317-bcbd-d2c7b7bf754e" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186093806}] :question/id #uuid "d119ffe6-ff07-57d9-a572-ef180eb72f6c" :question/math-academy-id "q-11414" :question/problem "Given that $f(x) = (3x^{5} - 4x^{2})^{3}$, find $f^{′}(x)$."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186097259 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a9bd8424-cd28-568d-ba73-3aa7ea472675" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "- ((4x + 3))/(\\sqrt{(2x^{2} + 3x)^{3}})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186095479} {:answer/id #uuid "38abde75-2551-5055-a50e-d986efdfd2f4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(4x + 3)/(\\sqrt{(2x^{2} + 3x)^{3}})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186095480} {:answer/id #uuid "3c031738-7346-5134-8117-9aee7a7c7253" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "- ((4x + 3))/(2\\sqrt{(2x^{2} + 3x)^{3}})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186095481} {:answer/id #uuid "5ee719f1-7581-568a-8881-2ef944019149" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "- ((4x + 3))/(\\sqrt[3]{(2x^{2} + 3x)^{2}})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186095482} {:answer/id #uuid "7ef48c55-c62b-518e-a659-3edcd2ea1e5d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "- ((8x + 6))/(\\sqrt{(2x^{2} + 3x)^{3}})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186095483}] :answer-field/id #uuid "7bd41183-7068-5455-b0e0-8cdceb8831b6" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186095478}] :question/id #uuid "2da730c2-fcbd-535d-85d1-2014785f31eb" :question/math-academy-id "q-48850" :question/problem "Find $f^{′}(x)$ for $f(x) = \\frac{2}{\\sqrt{2x^{2} + 3x}}$."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186244041 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3b70de32-5fa9-5056-93ca-35a9463faadb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5(3x+4{)}^{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186241712} {:answer/id #uuid "3ec14f92-c87e-5787-a89f-47a342d4236d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3(3x+4{)}^{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186241739} {:answer/id #uuid "5d608479-e85c-5eaa-9d74-3ea2e83804de" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "20(3x+4{)}^{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186242022} {:answer/id #uuid "8a1ebdf8-e7b7-53d7-92ca-ae1bc7bccfdd" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "9(3x+4{)}^{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186242395} {:answer/id #uuid "9a91c92b-ab22-552d-a427-31624937e728" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "15(3x+4{)}^{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186242530}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "9a91c92b-ab22-552d-a427-31624937e728" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "15(3x+4{)}^{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186242530} :answer-field/id #uuid "2cdfb9ab-5d97-59c1-90d9-2ac765b9ee1d" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186243521}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "5ffe447f-f2a2-5d58-986c-0fb23858e18e" :question/math-academy-id "q-107293" :question/problem "Given that $f(x)={(3x+4)}^{5},$ find ${f}^{′}(x).$" :question/worked-solution "Let $g(x)=3x+4$ and $f(g(x))=g(x{)}^{5}.$ \n\nDifferentiating, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(g(x)) & =({g}^{5}{)}^{′}=5{g}^{4}, \\\\ {g}^{′}(x) & =(3x+4{)}^{′}=3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, using the chain rule, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & ={f}^{′}(g(x))\\cdot {g}^{′}(x) \\\\  & =5{g}^{4}\\cdot 3 \\\\  & =15(3x+4{)}^{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186317327 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "bc8b5aee-000a-5a8d-818f-e20c5debd3ff" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "20(4x+3)^{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316184}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "bc8b5aee-000a-5a8d-818f-e20c5debd3ff" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "20(4x+3)^{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316184} :answer-field/id #uuid "625a79d7-54d7-570b-aa7c-729d582ac660" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316778}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "9ebc59fe-5ccc-59b1-9757-9c760459c2ae" :question/math-academy-id "q-342804" :question/problem "Given that $f(x)={(4x+3)}^{5},$ find ${f}^{′}(x).$\n\n${f}^{′}(x)=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "Let $g(x)=4x+3$ and $f(g(x))=g(x{)}^{5}.$ \n\nDifferentiating, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(g(x)) & =({g}^{5}{)}^{′}=5{g}^{4}, \\\\ {g}^{′}(x) & =(4x+3{)}^{′}=4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, using the chain rule, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & ={f}^{′}(g(x))\\cdot {g}^{′}(x) \\\\  & =5{g}^{4}\\cdot (4) \\\\  & =20(4x+3{)}^{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186317333 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8817f05b-018c-5853-b6cc-e00880617a94" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5{x}^{4}-16{x}^{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186315880}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "8817f05b-018c-5853-b6cc-e00880617a94" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5{x}^{4}-16{x}^{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186315880} :answer-field/id #uuid "e5ab2d60-fecd-56b2-a6fc-370a03381af0" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186317093}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "2fb4db78-694f-5845-af11-624db7bcff81" :question/math-academy-id "q-342875" :question/problem "Given that $f(x)={({x}^{5}-4{x}^{4})}^{2},$ find ${f}^{′}(x).$\n\n${f}^{′}(x)=2({x}^{5}-4{x}^{4})($\n{{field-1}}\n$)$" :question/worked-solution "Let $g(x)={x}^{5}-4{x}^{4}$ and $f(g(x))=g(x)^{2}.$ \n\nDifferentiating, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(g(x)) & =({g}^{2})^{′}=2g, \\\\ {g}^{′}(x) & =({x}^{5}-4{x}^{4})^{′}=5{x}^{4}-16{x}^{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, using the chain rule, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & ={f}^{′}(g(x))\\cdot {g}^{′}(x) \\\\  & =2g\\cdot (5{x}^{4}-16{x}^{3}) \\\\  & =2({x}^{5}-4{x}^{4})(\\,5{x}^{4}-16{x}^{3}\\,).\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186317335 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "54037730-2600-58cb-b40d-648fd184a076" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{5}{2\\sqrt{5x-4}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186315600}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "54037730-2600-58cb-b40d-648fd184a076" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{5}{2\\sqrt{5x-4}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186315600} :answer-field/id #uuid "0f9abe6a-bb3b-59b2-9f07-73fdde4dcc36" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316583}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "6a140a75-4d09-557b-baa7-d7202838dd2f" :question/math-academy-id "q-342883" :question/problem "Given that $f(x)=\\sqrt{5x-4},$ find ${f}^{′}(x).$\n\n${f}^{′}(x)=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "Let $g(x)=5x-4$ and $f(g(x))=\\sqrt{g(x)}.$ \n\nDifferentiating, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(g(x)) & ={(\\sqrt{g})}^{′}=\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{g}}, \\\\ {g}^{′}(x) & =(5x-4)^{′}=5.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, using the chain rule, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & ={f}^{′}(g(x))\\cdot {g}^{′}(x) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{g}}\\cdot 5 \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{2\\sqrt{5x-4}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186317336 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "d665e027-6ab2-5ec3-82b7-84f4dddc219b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x-4{x}^{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316315}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "d665e027-6ab2-5ec3-82b7-84f4dddc219b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x-4{x}^{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316315} :answer-field/id #uuid "e1297ade-d13c-5373-bff2-2ee35469ee63" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186317084}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "49fe03e2-8c48-5c16-b76a-8505b327ec2d" :question/math-academy-id "q-342891" :question/problem "Given that $f(x)=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2{x}^{4}-{x}^{2}+5}},$ find ${f}^{′}(x).$\n\n$\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{(2{x}^{4}-{x}^{2}+5{)}^{3}}}($\n{{field-1}}\n$)$" :question/worked-solution "Let $g(x)=2{x}^{4}-{x}^{2}+5$ and $f(g(x))=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{g(x)}}.$ \n\nDifferentiating, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(g(x)) & ={(\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{g}})}^{′}=-\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{{g}^{3}}}, \\\\ {g}^{′}(x) & =(2{x}^{4}-{x}^{2}+5{)}^{′}=8{x}^{3}-2x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, using the chain rule, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & ={f}^{′}(g(x))\\cdot {g}^{′}(x) \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{{g}^{3}}}\\cdot (8{x}^{3}-2x) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{(2{x}^{4}-{x}^{2}+5{)}^{3}}}(\\,x-4{x}^{3}\\,).\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186339021 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2c5aa34c-8035-515e-9a22-0910ed15b1fe" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "32(16x-7{)}^{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186336452} {:answer/id #uuid "8dec9f04-7eaf-5206-a8d3-7559c929a68c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "16(8x-7{)}^{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186337312} {:answer/id #uuid "be24eea7-2334-5d10-9f49-6df8807cf6b2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "16(16x-7{)}^{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186337721} {:answer/id #uuid "d7e1b6c9-7b2d-5b89-b492-99eacd8fa4bb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "8(8x-7{)}^{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186337907} {:answer/id #uuid "fb4036bb-195b-5a9c-b701-d3dee3bfbc0b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "32(8x-7{)}^{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186338239}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "fb4036bb-195b-5a9c-b701-d3dee3bfbc0b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "32(8x-7{)}^{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186338239} :answer-field/id #uuid "37b56870-5595-52b0-ae23-7f96f4a87f21" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186338377}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "217bdf0c-2bc6-585f-ac52-fad4aec9092f" :question/math-academy-id "q-71954" :question/problem "Given that $f(x)={(8x-7)}^{4},$ find ${f}^{′}(x).$" :question/worked-solution "Let $g(x)=8x-7$ and $f(g(x))=g(x{)}^{4}.$ \n\nDifferentiating, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(g(x)) & =({g}^{4}{)}^{′}=4{g}^{3}, \\\\ {g}^{′}(x) & =(8x-7{)}^{′}=8.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, using the chain rule, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & ={f}^{′}(g(x))\\cdot {g}^{′}(x) \\\\  & =4{g}^{3}\\cdot (8) \\\\  & =32(8x-7{)}^{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186339023 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2d2f6517-e258-513a-b912-2250e7c54e17" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-15(2+5x{)}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186336461} {:answer/id #uuid "3b2c8729-231c-5b49-a873-246a32aa4645" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-15(2-5x{)}^{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186336588} {:answer/id #uuid "77d335ef-89db-5392-8e96-2e5663d37f72" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "15(2+5x{)}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186337111} {:answer/id #uuid "84c34e15-f7f7-51bd-91ad-be1849bcac42" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "15(2-5x{)}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186337229} {:answer/id #uuid "ba6755f1-59bc-5601-b289-66317d2b0d63" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-15(2-5x{)}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186337682}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "ba6755f1-59bc-5601-b289-66317d2b0d63" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-15(2-5x{)}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186337682} :answer-field/id #uuid "20dca297-5492-547a-957c-959f9e7520f3" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186338339}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "ccad926b-6344-5bec-99bc-507d50cda1e3" :question/math-academy-id "q-71956" :question/problem "Given that $f(x)={(2-5x)}^{3},$ find ${f}^{′}(x).$" :question/worked-solution "Let $g(x)=2-5x$ and $f(g(x))=g(x{)}^{3}.$ \n\nDifferentiating, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(g(x)) & =({g}^{3}{)}^{′}=3{g}^{2}, \\\\ {g}^{′}(x) & =(2-5x{)}^{′}=-5.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, using the chain rule, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & ={f}^{′}(g(x))\\cdot {g}^{′}(x) \\\\  & =3{g}^{2}\\cdot (-5) \\\\  & =-15(2-5x{)}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] :knowledge-point/title "Differentiating a Function Using the Chain Rule and Prime Notation"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186100888 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186100725 :question/id #uuid "e092e191-84ac-5d9f-b0db-94d628a6154e" :question/math-academy-id "e-525" :question/problem "Consider the following differential equation.\n\n$$\ny' = -\\dfrac{y}{x}, \\qquad x \\neq 0\n$$\n\n \n\nLet $f(x) = \\dfrac{1}{x}.$ Which of the following statements are true?\n\n- $f'(x) = -\\dfrac{1}{x^2}$\n\n- Substituting $y = f(x)$ and $y' = f'(x)$ into the differential equation gives a true statement\n\n- The function $y=f(x)$ is a solution of the differential equation" :question/worked-solution "For a function $f(x)$ to be a solution to a differential equation, it must satisfy the equation for all values of $x$ in the equation's domain.\n\nWith that in mind, let's check each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. Differentiating $f(x) = \\dfrac{1}{x},$ we get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & ={(\\frac{1}{x})}^{′} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement II is true. Substituting $y = \\dfrac{1}{x}$ and $y' = -\\dfrac{1}{x^2}$ into the original equation, we get a true statement:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & \\overset{?}{=}-\\frac{y}{x} \\\\ -\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}} & \\overset{?}{=}-\\frac{(\\frac{1}{x})}{x} \\\\ -\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}} & =-\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}}\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement III is true. Since statement II is true, the function $y = \\dfrac{1}{x}$ is a solution to our differential equation.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I, II, and III.\""} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "201edc93-37b4-556a-aaea-4fcf9a01077a" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048593 :topic/math-academy-id 35 :topic/title "The Power Rule for Differentiation"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049592 :topic/math-academy-id 3215 :topic/title "Introduction to Differential Equations"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050271 :topic/math-academy-id 1007 :topic/title "The Chain Rule With Exponential Functions"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050690 :topic/math-academy-id 1116 :topic/title "Differentiating Logarithmic Functions"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050789 :topic/math-academy-id 305 :topic/title "The Chain Rule With Trigonometric Functions"}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186101155 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "27a52cd0-ac03-5c8d-a617-3cb7b0126fd2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186269616} {:answer/id #uuid "5df0e9d5-2b2c-5d79-ab84-ca87885e2473" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186270104} {:answer/id #uuid "701a5dbc-2e62-5de3-945b-e34b10bc5f39" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186270261} {:answer/id #uuid "cccad23a-acfd-50dc-8b43-cda593fadb2a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186271065} {:answer/id #uuid "d430c687-b7e8-58dd-98eb-e50553cad6a9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186271134}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "5df0e9d5-2b2c-5d79-ab84-ca87885e2473" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186270104} :answer-field/id #uuid "64b40622-fbf9-5a3e-8034-8af1ed1a214f" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186271687}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "0b2e480d-7f17-59ba-a02c-a786007bf39b" :question/math-academy-id "q-140631" :question/problem "Consider the following differential equation.\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=\\frac{4y}{x},\\,x\\ne 0\n$$\n\n \n\nLet $f(x)={x}^{4}.$ Which of the following statements are true?\n\n- ${f}^{′}(x)=5{x}^{5}.$\n- Substituting $y=f(x)$ and ${y}^{′}={f}^{′}(x)$ into the differential equation gives a true statement.\n- The function $y=f(x)$ is a solution of the differential equation." :question/worked-solution "For a function $f(x)$ to be a solution to a differential equation, it must satisfy the equation for all values of $x$ in the equation's domain.\n\nWith that in mind, let's check each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement I is false. Differentiating $f(x)={x}^{4},$ we get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & =({x}^{4})^{′} \\\\  & =4{x}^{3} \\\\  & \\ne 5{x}^{5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement II is true. Substituting $y={x}^{4}$ and ${y}^{′}=4{x}^{3}$ into the original equation, we get a true statement:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & \\overset{?}{=}\\frac{4y}{x} \\\\ (4{x}^{3}) & \\overset{?}{=}\\frac{4({x}^{4})}{x} \\\\ 4{x}^{3} & =4{x}^{3}\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement III is true. Since statement II is true, the function $y={x}^{4}$ is a solution to our differential equation.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II and III only.\""} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186101157 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2c14d1ed-5752-5ac0-8ba1-5a643f5dfc35" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186269665} {:answer/id #uuid "94fc8685-d2f0-5ef2-a7ab-e1b35ff78eb3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186270574} {:answer/id #uuid "b0466bd8-b819-5e7b-b7cd-9e56708baddc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186270841} {:answer/id #uuid "e12cb1db-96c6-55e9-af73-73a5530aeb77" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186271246} {:answer/id #uuid "e2e78926-ddd2-5989-b665-eac77287af5f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186271264}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "e12cb1db-96c6-55e9-af73-73a5530aeb77" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186271246} :answer-field/id #uuid "6609800c-a1ac-5b87-bb69-03288bbe55d7" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186271691}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "47b66471-b70f-5509-8368-6a6674b74447" :question/math-academy-id "q-141078" :question/problem "Consider the following differential equation.\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=2y\n$$\n\n \n\nLet $f(x)={e}^{2x}.$ Which of the following statements are true?\n\n- ${f}^{′}(x)=2{e}^{2x}$\n- Substituting $y=f(x)$ and ${y}^{′}={f}^{′}(x)$ into the differential equation gives a true statement\n- The function $y=f(x)$ is a solution of the differential equation" :question/worked-solution "For a function $f(x)$ to be a solution to a differential equation, it must satisfy the equation for all values of $x$ in the equation's domain.\n\nWith that in mind, let's check each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. Differentiating $f(x)={e}^{2x},$ we get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & ={({e}^{2x})}^{′} \\\\  & =2{e}^{2x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement II is true. Substituting $y={e}^{2x}$ and ${y}^{′}=2{e}^{2x}$ into the original equation, we get a true statement:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & \\overset{?}{=}2y \\\\ 2{e}^{2x} & =2{e}^{2x}\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement III is true. Since statement II is true, the function $y={e}^{2x}$ is a solution to our differential equation.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I, II, and III.\""} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186272196 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "34270485-5bff-52bf-ae41-08b1bee1a1d0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "None" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186269746} {:answer/id #uuid "389a0c65-5adf-5c0f-9d62-a0d6a6c0821b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186269783} {:answer/id #uuid "5531b938-deac-5f0d-96bc-e4236e777318" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186270026} {:answer/id #uuid "8e825cdc-2aaf-56bb-9c28-3d7dfac2e941" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186270505} {:answer/id #uuid "d0121132-7103-5393-80a4-507acd8c4184" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186271097}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "34270485-5bff-52bf-ae41-08b1bee1a1d0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "None" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186269746} :answer-field/id #uuid "1beb641a-e76c-5ac9-9803-42dc3f2e4a6e" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186271559}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "5c28aa3d-73cf-552e-9e05-dfc5bcb67151" :question/math-academy-id "q-141080" :question/problem "Consider the following differential equation.\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=2y\n$$\n\n \n\nLet $f(x)=\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x.$ Which of the following statements are true?\n\n- ${f}^{′}(x)=2\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x$\n- Substituting $y=f(x)$ and ${y}^{′}={f}^{′}(x)$ into the differential equation gives a true statement\n- The function $y=f(x)$ is a solution of the differential equation" :question/worked-solution "For a function $f(x)$ to be a solution to a differential equation, it must satisfy the equation for all values of $x$ in the equation's domain.\n\nWith that in mind, let's check each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement I is false. Differentiating $f(x)=\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x,$ we get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & ={(\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x)}^{′} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot 2\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x \\\\  & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x \\\\  & \\ne 2\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement II is false. Substituting $y=\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x$ and ${y}^{′}=\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x$ into the original equation, we get a false statement:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & \\overset{?}{=}2y \\\\ \\operatorname{cos}⁡2x & \\overset{?}{=}2(\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x) \\\\ \\operatorname{cos}⁡2x & \\ne \\operatorname{sin}⁡2x\\,\\times \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement III is false. Since statement II is false, the function $y=\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x$ is not a solution to our differential equation.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"None.\""} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186272197 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "65720cff-ac67-573c-b355-c946b6d47e2b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186270173} {:answer/id #uuid "771372c6-f984-51d7-819d-583b075d5f61" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186270320} {:answer/id #uuid "c019a28a-dcd7-5e5a-a2e2-15357e719fa8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186270971} {:answer/id #uuid "c724b081-8081-5843-86e5-29e06d070d0e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186271023} {:answer/id #uuid "de5a2a25-e529-5bdd-9c7e-749f5a48a47a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186271219}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "c724b081-8081-5843-86e5-29e06d070d0e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186271023} :answer-field/id #uuid "48e408fe-eb76-5085-b71a-96030d52904b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186271640}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "82065c87-2fb6-51b8-8d32-9c57d0719fb4" :question/math-academy-id "q-141081" :question/problem "Consider the following differential equation.\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=y\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x\n$$\n\n \n\nLet $f(x)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x.$ Which of the following statements are true?\n\n- ${f}^{′}(x)=-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x$\n- Substituting $y=f(x)$ and ${y}^{′}={f}^{′}(x)$ into the differential equation gives a true statement\n- The function $y=f(x)$ is a solution of the differential equation" :question/worked-solution "For a function $f(x)$ to be a solution to a differential equation, it must satisfy the equation for all values of $x$ in the equation's domain.\n\nWith that in mind, let's check each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. Differentiating $f(x)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x,$ we get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & ={(\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x)}^{′} \\\\  & =-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement II is false. Substituting $y=\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x$ and ${y}^{′}=-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x$ into the original equation, we get a false statement:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & \\overset{?}{=}y\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x \\\\ -2\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x & \\overset{?}{=}\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x\\cdot \\operatorname{cos}⁡2x \\\\ -2\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x & \\ne {cos}^{2}⁡2x\\,\\times \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement III is false. Since statement II is false, the function $y=\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x$ is not a solution to our differential equation.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\""} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186272198 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1fcca0f3-a734-5821-a06d-f8343e70ea2d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186269534} {:answer/id #uuid "6d5dd004-13d5-581c-aad4-bee0a7e60ec2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186270239} {:answer/id #uuid "73a31251-c78f-5484-b652-d7fc86f10971" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186270290} {:answer/id #uuid "93bacff5-8f98-5a78-9a0a-9604f419972e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186270560} {:answer/id #uuid "fa2bf753-807f-5720-a657-2805a9668a72" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186271456}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "1fcca0f3-a734-5821-a06d-f8343e70ea2d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186269534} :answer-field/id #uuid "0a79a925-e351-511b-b352-4a48c43fdc1e" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186271524}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "9b7a88f7-a074-5f69-9224-f1585f849cc4" :question/math-academy-id "q-141082" :question/problem "Consider the following differential equation.\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=\\frac{1}{{e}^{y}}\n$$\n\n \n\nLet $f(x)=\\operatorname{ln}⁡x.$ Which of the following statements are true?\n\n- ${f}^{′}(x)=\\frac{1}{x}$\n- Substituting $y=f(x)$ and ${y}^{′}={f}^{′}(x)$ into the differential equation gives a true statement\n- The function $y=f(x)$ is a solution of the differential equation" :question/worked-solution "For a function $f(x)$ to be a solution to a differential equation, it must satisfy the equation for all values of $x$ in the equation's domain.\n\nWith that in mind, let's check each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. Differentiating $f(x)=\\operatorname{ln}⁡x,$ we get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & ={(\\operatorname{ln}⁡x)}^{′} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement II is true. Substituting $y=\\operatorname{ln}⁡x$ and ${y}^{′}=\\frac{1}{x}$ into the original equation, we get a true statement:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & \\overset{?}{=}\\frac{1}{{e}^{y}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{x} & \\overset{?}{=}\\frac{1}{{e}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡x}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{x} & =\\frac{1}{x}\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement III is true. Since statement II is true, the function $y=\\operatorname{ln}⁡x$ is a solution to our differential equation.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I, II, and III.\""} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186272199 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "74ae0ac0-cbd7-5e19-9e62-18c4e30401ad" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186270299} {:answer/id #uuid "7d855e8a-1d57-5875-8d6d-12d0951b8e3c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186270369} {:answer/id #uuid "7f7151bb-1ac2-5705-85fc-5081794b458e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186270380} {:answer/id #uuid "c08033e6-c809-5d66-be80-0f4095dc7570" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186270973} {:answer/id #uuid "f54e5a37-096a-5380-9441-b692b0890ac8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "None" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186271423}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "f54e5a37-096a-5380-9441-b692b0890ac8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "None" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186271423} :answer-field/id #uuid "574997d4-2cf3-5549-972e-17ab6fe6132d" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186271667}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "c4402732-29b4-541b-a6e6-a372fd33bf97" :question/math-academy-id "q-141084" :question/problem "Consider the following differential equation.\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=\\frac{y}{{x}^{2}},\\,x\\ne 0\n$$\n\n \n\nLet $f(x)=-\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}}.$ Which of the following statements are true?\n\n- ${f}^{′}(x)=-\\frac{2}{{x}^{3}}$\n- Substituting $y=f(x)$ and ${y}^{′}={f}^{′}(x)$ into the differential equation gives a true statement\n- The function $y=f(x)$ is a solution of the differential equation" :question/worked-solution "For a function $f(x)$ to be a solution to a differential equation, it must satisfy the equation for all values of $x$ in the equation's domain.\n\nWith that in mind, let's check each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement I is false. Differentiating $f(x)=-\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}},$ we get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & ={(-\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}})}^{′} \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{{x}^{3}} \\\\  & \\ne -\\frac{2}{{x}^{3}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement II is false. Substituting $y=-\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}}$ and ${y}^{′}=\\frac{2}{{x}^{3}}$ into the original equation, we get a false statement:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & \\overset{?}{=}\\frac{y}{{x}^{2}} \\\\ \\frac{2}{{x}^{3}} & \\overset{?}{=}\\frac{(-\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}})}{{x}^{2}} \\\\ \\frac{2}{{x}^{3}} & \\ne -\\frac{1}{{x}^{4}}\\,\\times \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement III is false. Since statement II is false, the function $y=-\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}}$ is not a solution to our differential equation.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"None.\""} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186272200 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "027f07cc-cf92-57e1-a7dd-52d7f06d98a9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186269292} {:answer/id #uuid "28cefc3d-d321-5b03-a62f-d8e2472c48f1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "None" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186269636} {:answer/id #uuid "2fb96c78-c8a0-51e1-a8a5-0e898bc94a78" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186269696} {:answer/id #uuid "88d6f270-4f72-5d1a-bb5b-8fbb6eb96434" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186270449} {:answer/id #uuid "b73d03e8-8fba-5412-a8cc-88b5936c079c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186270892}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "28cefc3d-d321-5b03-a62f-d8e2472c48f1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "None" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186269636} :answer-field/id #uuid "a051e209-09e1-5e9e-b803-9d96c9cf127a" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186271797}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "33a5a8c7-40f0-5353-a590-88087e77753a" :question/math-academy-id "q-141086" :question/problem "Consider the following differential equation.\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=-3y\n$$\n\n \n\nLet $f(x)=3{e}^{-x}.$ Which of the following statements are true?\n\n- ${f}^{′}(x)=3{e}^{-x}$\n- Substituting $y=f(x)$ and ${y}^{′}={f}^{′}(x)$ into the differential equation gives a true statement\n- The function $y=f(x)$ is a solution of the differential equation" :question/worked-solution "For a function $f(x)$ to be a solution to a differential equation, it must satisfy the equation for all values of $x$ in the equation's domain.\n\nWith that in mind, let's check each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement I is false. Differentiating $f(x)=3{e}^{-x},$ we get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & ={(3{e}^{-x})}^{′} \\\\  & =-3{e}^{-x} \\\\  & \\ne 3{e}^{-x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement II is false. Substituting $y=3{e}^{-x}$ and ${y}^{′}=-3{e}^{-x}$ into the original equation, we get a false statement:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & \\overset{?}{=}-3y \\\\ -3{e}^{-x} & \\overset{?}{=}-3(3{e}^{-x}) \\\\ -3{e}^{-x} & \\ne -9{e}^{-x}\\,\\times \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement III is false. Since statement II is false, the function $y=3{e}^{-x}$ is not a solution to our differential equation.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"None.\""} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186272201 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "496e15c7-53c7-579c-8873-1e0eda230e59" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186269937} {:answer/id #uuid "5806fb9e-afea-5d8f-9ce2-c668e570e705" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186270046} {:answer/id #uuid "ba3be53d-e686-5999-8cae-d77e8bfdbcc8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186270914} {:answer/id #uuid "dbf24249-8330-5344-9a68-ed06fb6b088d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186271194} {:answer/id #uuid "dee82fb9-fbfd-5840-bc3a-4c4b47cb6275" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "None" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186271223}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "496e15c7-53c7-579c-8873-1e0eda230e59" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186269937} :answer-field/id #uuid "e1cdc1fa-0584-5f35-a9ea-2a774691d9b3" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186271906}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "9c8904e1-5f17-5951-8dbc-61b76cd0babc" :question/math-academy-id "q-141088" :question/problem "Consider the following differential equation.\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=\\frac{4x}{{e}^{y/2}}\n$$\n\n \n\nLet $f(x)=4\\operatorname{ln}⁡x.$ Which of the following statements are true?\n\n- ${f}^{′}(x)=\\frac{1}{4x}$\n- Substituting $y=f(x)$ and ${y}^{′}={f}^{′}(x)$ into the differential equation gives a true statement\n- The function $y=f(x)$ is a solution of the differential equation" :question/worked-solution "For a function $f(x)$ to be a solution to a differential equation, it must satisfy the equation for all values of $x$ in the equation's domain.\n\nWith that in mind, let's check each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement I is false. Differentiating $f(x)=4\\operatorname{ln}⁡x,$ we get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & ={(4\\operatorname{ln}⁡x)}^{′} \\\\  & =\\frac{4}{x} \\\\  & \\ne \\frac{1}{4x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement II is true. Substituting $y=4\\operatorname{ln}⁡x$ and ${y}^{′}=\\frac{4}{x}$ into the original equation, we get a true statement:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & \\overset{?}{=}\\frac{4x}{{e}^{y/2}} \\\\ \\frac{4}{x} & \\overset{?}{=}\\frac{4x}{{e}^{(4\\operatorname{ln}⁡x)/2}} \\\\ \\frac{4}{x} & \\overset{?}{=}\\frac{4x}{{e}^{2\\operatorname{ln}⁡x}} \\\\ \\frac{4}{x} & \\overset{?}{=}\\frac{4x}{{e}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡{x}^{2}}} \\\\ \\frac{4}{x} & \\overset{?}{=}\\frac{4x}{{x}^{2}} \\\\ \\frac{4}{x} & =\\frac{4}{x}\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement III is true. Since statement II is true, the function $y=4\\operatorname{ln}⁡x$ is a solution to our differential equation.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II and III only.\""} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186272202 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1da8ca83-9810-5e03-afc4-c10c787af01c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186269514} {:answer/id #uuid "9cdd200c-7408-5659-8fe2-7f44e245f0d1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186270653} {:answer/id #uuid "a2061503-cef9-50bf-a85a-ef2c605b3879" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "None" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186270708} {:answer/id #uuid "e813d0ad-d2f2-5d00-b765-d4005a112175" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186271297} {:answer/id #uuid "eb031019-568e-5c95-9847-afd852126e06" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186271329}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "9cdd200c-7408-5659-8fe2-7f44e245f0d1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186270653} :answer-field/id #uuid "e2f29670-922a-5956-81fd-e1a1924ed0c0" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186271911}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "8806c061-892c-5fd2-baf1-cd903aa5ea31" :question/math-academy-id "q-141089" :question/problem "Consider the following differential equation.\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=\\frac{2y}{x},\\,x\\ne 0\n$$\n\n \n\nLet $f(x)=-3{x}^{2}.$ Which of the following statements are true?\n\n- ${f}^{′}(x)=-5x$\n- Substituting $y=f(x)$ and ${y}^{′}={f}^{′}(x)$ into the differential equation gives a true statement\n- The function $y=f(x)$ is a solution of the differential equation" :question/worked-solution "For a function $f(x)$ to be a solution to a differential equation, it must satisfy the equation for all values of $x$ in the equation's domain.\n\nWith that in mind, let's check each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement I is false. Differentiating $f(x)=-3{x}^{2},$ we get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & ={(-3{x}^{2})}^{′} \\\\  & =-3\\cdot 2x \\\\  & =-6x \\\\  & \\ne -5x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement II is true. Substituting $y=-3{x}^{2}$ and ${y}^{′}=-6x$ into the original equation, we get a true statement:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & \\overset{?}{=}\\frac{2y}{x} \\\\ -6x & \\overset{?}{=}\\frac{2(-3{x}^{2})}{x} \\\\ -6x & =-6x\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement III is true. Since statement II is true, the function $y=-3{x}^{2}$ is a solution to our differential equation.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II and III only.\""}] :knowledge-point/title "Identifying a Solution to a First-Order Differential Equation"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186100889 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186100713 :question/id #uuid "cbd0cb3d-cf0f-54db-8cfc-c938e463e4f8" :question/math-academy-id "e-5087" :question/problem "Find the Cartesian equation of the curve defined parametrically by\n\n$$\nx=2t^2, \\,\\,\\, y=t^2+1, \\quad t \\geq 0.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "To find the Cartesian equation, we eliminate $t.$ First we use the $x$ equation to solve for $t^2\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\nx=2t^2 \\quad \\Rightarrow\\quad  t^2 = \\dfrac{x}{2}\n$$\n\nNow substituting $t^2=\\dfrac{x}{2}$ into our expression for $y,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & ={t}^{2}+1 \\\\  & =\\frac{x}{2}+1 \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}x+1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo the Cartesian equation of the given curve is $y=\\dfrac{1}{2}x+1.$\n\nFinally, since $t \\geq0$ and $x=2t^2,$ we must have $x \\geq 0.$\n\nSo, the complete Cartesian equation is\n\n$$\ny=\\dfrac{1}{2}x+1, \\quad x \\geq 0 .\n$$"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "79107609-77e6-55e9-970d-da63c39daf0a" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048293 :topic/math-academy-id 803 :topic/title "Graphing Curves Defined Parametrically"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048760 :topic/math-academy-id 651 :topic/title "Solving Linear Equations by Clearing a Rational Expression"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048784 :topic/math-academy-id 116 :topic/title "Solving Radical Equations"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048826 :topic/math-academy-id 487 :topic/title "Solving Systems of Equations by Substitution"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050310 :topic/math-academy-id 3739 :topic/title "Adding Rational Expressions With No Common Factors in the Denominator"}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186101207 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "28b55e3d-922e-5fd5-a435-05886bb984e3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=-{(\\frac{1+2x}{x})}^{2},\\,x\\ne 0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186272749} {:answer/id #uuid "8a657071-1650-5d6c-b5c4-92125994bb16" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y={(\\frac{1-2x}{x})}^{2},\\,x\\ne 0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186273580} {:answer/id #uuid "94ae029c-717d-594a-94b3-99e945ac9df3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y={(\\frac{-1-2x}{x})}^{2},\\,x\\ne 0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186273683} {:answer/id #uuid "e5c8491d-ffbc-5e21-af51-3dd2104b993d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y={(\\frac{1+2x}{x})}^{2},\\,x\\ne 0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186274417} {:answer/id #uuid "f9e82ab8-5078-5eff-8d80-7c402c1a0adc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=-{(\\frac{-1+2x}{x})}^{2},\\,x\\ne 0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186274577}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "e5c8491d-ffbc-5e21-af51-3dd2104b993d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y={(\\frac{1+2x}{x})}^{2},\\,x\\ne 0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186274417} :answer-field/id #uuid "67afa213-144b-5939-9e4e-014024f2077a" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186274813}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "5acf88e6-b052-5b29-9849-a312529836f0" :question/math-academy-id "q-15678" :question/problem "Find the Cartesian equation of the curve defined parametrically by\n\n$$\nx=\\frac{1}{t-2},\\,\\,\\,y={t}^{2},\\,t\\ne 2.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "To find the Cartesian equation, we eliminate $t.$ First, we use the $x$ equation to solve for $t:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =\\frac{1}{t-2} \\\\ x(t-2) & =1 \\\\ t-2 & =\\frac{1}{x} \\\\ t & =\\frac{1}{x}+2 \\\\ t & =\\frac{1+2x}{x}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow substituting $t=\\frac{1+2x}{x}$ into our expression for $y={t}^{2},$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & ={(\\frac{1+2x}{x})}^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo the Cartesian equation of the given curve is $y={(\\frac{1+2x}{x})}^{2}.$\n\nFinally, since $t\\ne 2$ and $x=\\frac{1}{t-2},$ we must have $x\\ne 0.$\n\nSo, the complete Cartesian equation is\n\n$$\ny={(\\frac{1+2x}{x})}^{2},\\,x\\ne 0.\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186101210 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "85aa4d14-80d1-5752-8fd1-0411a67d3ba3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y = (x - 2)^{2}, x ∈ (- ∞, ∞)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186098596} {:answer/id #uuid "88ed67e5-8008-5e9a-bf16-7f21332088a2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y = x^{2}, x ∈ (- ∞, ∞)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186098597} {:answer/id #uuid "cb64b154-ef9a-5411-b1ee-b7338acfc194" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y = x + 2, x ∈ (- ∞, ∞)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186098598} {:answer/id #uuid "ec57c426-a179-55be-92bb-f8bfdbc1e791" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y = (x + 4)^{2}, x ∈ (- ∞, ∞)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186098599} {:answer/id #uuid "ed10e3fb-a8f9-5fb6-a491-53e533208b77" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y = (x + 2)^{2}, x ∈ (- ∞, ∞)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186098600}] :answer-field/id #uuid "553cbf31-db12-5ac2-aeb1-44860435fe50" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186098595}] :question/id #uuid "3962e387-0e50-5c09-a43b-458e9ce412cd" :question/math-academy-id "q-15755" :question/problem "Find the Cartesian equation of the curve defined parametrically by\n$x = t - 2, y = t^{2}, t ∈ (- ∞, ∞)$."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186237916 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "166d0a6c-05a8-5786-b48c-f4e93b1f3b0c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=-\\frac{1}{2}(x+3)^{2}(x-3)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186235955} {:answer/id #uuid "1dc3551c-3d67-5cc6-8438-b49e16bdf115" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=\\frac{1}{4}(x+3)^{2}(x-3)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186236011} {:answer/id #uuid "45c69b36-6d72-503c-9c36-09fae5919dba" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=-\\frac{1}{8}(x+3)^{2}(x-3)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186236267} {:answer/id #uuid "d00d1ba1-3166-54b8-b47f-c8a1f0daadff" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=-\\frac{1}{4}(x+3)(x-3)^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186237136} {:answer/id #uuid "f55f988f-ed06-5427-b54b-471e782de7ff" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=\\frac{1}{8}(x+3)(x-3)^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186237365}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "45c69b36-6d72-503c-9c36-09fae5919dba" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=-\\frac{1}{8}(x+3)^{2}(x-3)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186236267} :answer-field/id #uuid "8f11615a-9ff4-5a1c-97b7-4c67c3c73fb2" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186237627}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "440dd157-2982-5c1f-8661-23a8368ce029" :question/math-academy-id "q-102664" :question/problem "Find the Cartesian equation of the curve defined parametrically by\n\n$$\nx=1-2t,\\,\\,\\,y=(t-2)^{2}(t+1),\\,t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).\n$$" :question/worked-solution "To find the Cartesian equation, we eliminate $t.$ First, we use the $x$ equation to solve for $t:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x=1-2t\\,⇒\\,t=\\frac{1-x}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, substituting $t=\\frac{1-x}{2}$ into our expression for $y,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & ={(\\frac{1-x}{2}-2)}^{2}(\\frac{1-x}{2}+1) \\\\  & ={(\\frac{1-x-4}{2})}^{2}(\\frac{1-x+2}{2}) \\\\  & ={(\\frac{-x-3}{2})}^{2}(\\frac{3-x}{2}) \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{8}(x+3)^{2}(x-3).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo the Cartesian equation of the given curve is $y=-\\frac{1}{8}(x+3)^{2}(x-3).$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186237918 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "77633f7b-a4f6-5605-b197-300dedbccde8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=4x(x+5)(2x+1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186236567} {:answer/id #uuid "7b957847-885e-58d3-87c4-376c6913a3f1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=2(2x+1)^{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186236587} {:answer/id #uuid "878bf002-299b-523a-93bd-80e7642d9000" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=4x(2x+1)(2x-5)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186236663} {:answer/id #uuid "8b44f564-28f4-5786-9cf7-4b0382705e60" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=(2x+1)^{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186236690} {:answer/id #uuid "abe07822-f677-59f4-b923-e8aa2238417d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=2x(x-5)(2x+1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186236904}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "77633f7b-a4f6-5605-b197-300dedbccde8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=4x(x+5)(2x+1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186236567} :answer-field/id #uuid "b2e38393-de71-5a6b-b9b5-971eae7190b7" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186237668}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "44f36d19-593e-54ad-8307-e8844e507156" :question/math-academy-id "q-102668" :question/problem "Find the Cartesian equation of the curve defined parametrically by\n\n$$\nx=\\frac{t-5}{2},\\,\\,\\,y=({t}^{2}-25)(t-4),\\,t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).\n$$" :question/worked-solution "To find the Cartesian equation, we eliminate $t.$ First, we use the $x$ equation to solve for $t:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x=\\frac{t-5}{2}\\,⇒\\,t=2x+5\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, substituting $t=2x+5$ into our expression for $y,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =((2x+5)^{2}-25)(2x+5-4) \\\\  & =(4{x}^{2}+20x+25-25)(2x+1) \\\\  & =(4{x}^{2}+20x)(2x+1) \\\\  & =4x(x+5)(2x+1).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo the Cartesian equation of the given curve is $y=4x(x+5)(2x+1).$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186275353 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "20505f6c-76c3-5503-9aa4-58967d3492b7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=5{x}^{2}+x+2,\\,x\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty )" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186272676} {:answer/id #uuid "80f54d66-49b1-5053-b817-7ebbba893a52" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=-{x}^{2}+10x-24,\\,x\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty )" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186273492} {:answer/id #uuid "be1b076f-fa12-524f-9aeb-e359af08e3c6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=2{x}^{2}-5x-12,\\,x\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty )" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186274034} {:answer/id #uuid "db025224-4e97-5e03-8743-b73b379a2eea" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=-4{x}^{2}-2x+5,\\,x\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty )" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186274302} {:answer/id #uuid "e21deb99-1b3a-5d03-bd8b-47900ebde6d2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y={x}^{2}-10x+24,\\,x\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty )" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186274387}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "e21deb99-1b3a-5d03-bd8b-47900ebde6d2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y={x}^{2}-10x+24,\\,x\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty )" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186274387} :answer-field/id #uuid "1dcaa63f-4e24-5cd8-972a-532fc5338912" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186274690}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "be482fdb-4246-5b2d-93fc-c680fd3bc2e4" :question/math-academy-id "q-15653" :question/problem "Find the Cartesian equation of the curve defined parametrically by\n\n$$\nx=5-t,\\,\\,\\,y={t}^{2}-1,\\,t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).\n$$" :question/worked-solution "To find the Cartesian equation, we eliminate $t.$ First, we use the $x$ equation to solve for $t:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x=5-t\\,⇒\\,t=5-x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, substituting $t=5-x$ into our expression for $y,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =(5-x)^{2}-1 \\\\  & =25-10x+{x}^{2}-1 \\\\  & ={x}^{2}-10x+24\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo the Cartesian equation of the given curve is $y={x}^{2}-10x+24.$\n\nFinally, since $t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty )$ and $x=5-t,$ we must have $x\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).$\n\nSo, the complete Cartesian equation is\n\n$$\ny={x}^{2}-10x+24,\\,x\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186275354 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "20261641-fd84-5f77-86f9-34f0fa33a731" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=-x(3+{x}^{2}),\\,x\\ge 0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186272674} {:answer/id #uuid "24a93e7a-6bb2-5462-9150-db02feafe760" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=2{x}^{2}(9-{x}^{2}),\\,x\\ge 0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186272708} {:answer/id #uuid "43312442-bb1a-5f99-b317-fe4c73170b3c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=-2x(9-{x}^{2}),\\,x\\ge 0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186272963} {:answer/id #uuid "65eba75f-db0c-5c62-b886-977dc76e3ace" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y={x}^{2}(9-{x}^{2}),\\,x\\ge 0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186273260} {:answer/id #uuid "8180fde6-b73a-51b5-92cf-81508b0e34d4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y={x}^{2}(3+{x}^{2}),\\,x\\ge 0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186273495}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "65eba75f-db0c-5c62-b886-977dc76e3ace" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y={x}^{2}(9-{x}^{2}),\\,x\\ge 0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186273260} :answer-field/id #uuid "5d6adaab-7830-564f-ac4c-cf11efa896cf" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186274795}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "48edb5ee-f8fa-5b2d-bf8e-0422cc536872" :question/math-academy-id "q-15654" :question/problem "Find the Cartesian equation of the curve defined parametrically by\n\n$$\nx=\\sqrt{t},\\,\\,\\,y=t(9-t),\\,t\\ge 0.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "To find the Cartesian equation, we eliminate $t.$ First, we use the $x$ equation to solve for $t:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x=\\sqrt{t}\\,⇒\\,t={x}^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, substituting $t={x}^{2}$ into our expression for $y,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & ={x}^{2}(9-{x}^{2})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo the Cartesian equation of the given curve is $y={x}^{2}(9-{x}^{2}).$\n\nFinally, since $t\\ge 0$ and $x=\\sqrt{t},$ we must have $x\\ge 0.$\n\nSo, the complete Cartesian equation is\n\n$$\ny={x}^{2}(9-{x}^{2}),\\,x\\ge 0.\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186275366 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1d045052-a873-54a4-84e5-7bf4e6574118" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=\\frac{3x}{1+2x},\\,x\\ne -\\frac{1}{2},\\,0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186272644} {:answer/id #uuid "22c4e507-f7c6-5de7-b268-663b56128436" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=\\frac{x}{1-x},\\,\\,x\\ne 0,\\,1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186272694} {:answer/id #uuid "81222995-5d78-5bef-9adf-4e395c20b564" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=\\frac{x}{1+x},\\,\\,x\\ne -1,\\,0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186273494} {:answer/id #uuid "af021638-e540-5191-98d4-73961ba655c5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=\\frac{x}{1-3x},\\,x\\ne 0,\\,\\frac{1}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186273899} {:answer/id #uuid "db25591c-9dfe-5ed2-9b3e-5d90e2274d32" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=\\frac{3x}{1-2x},\\,x\\ne 0,\\,\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186274304}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "af021638-e540-5191-98d4-73961ba655c5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=\\frac{x}{1-3x},\\,x\\ne 0,\\,\\frac{1}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186273899} :answer-field/id #uuid "5ae43d0f-3f4e-5d8a-ae9f-f9aea258ba0a" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186274789}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "d29a5f16-5e4f-594a-98bd-3d918fb3b468" :question/math-academy-id "q-15677" :question/problem "Find the Cartesian equation of the curve defined parametrically by\n\n$$\nx=\\frac{1}{t+1},\\,\\,\\,y=\\frac{1}{t-2},\\,t\\ne -1,\\,2.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "To find the Cartesian equation, we eliminate $t.$ First, we use the $x$ equation to solve for $t:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x=\\frac{1}{t+1}\\,⇒\\,t=\\frac{1}{x}-1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow substituting $t=\\frac{1}{x}-1$ into our expression for $y$, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =\\frac{1}{((\\frac{1}{x}-1)-2)} \\\\ y & =\\frac{1}{(\\frac{1}{x}-3)} \\\\ y & =\\frac{1}{(\\frac{1-3x}{x})} \\\\ y & =\\frac{x}{1-3x}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo the Cartesian equation of the given curve is $y=\\frac{x}{1-3x}.$\n\nFinally, since $t\\ne -1,\\,2$ and $x=\\frac{1}{t+1},$ we must have $x\\ne 0,\\,\\frac{1}{3}.$\n\nSo, the complete Cartesian equation is\n\n$$\ny=\\frac{x}{1-3x},\\,x\\ne 0,\\,\\frac{1}{3}.\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186292916 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "f98dd494-7f2f-57dc-af90-42867eb57511" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{x}^{2}-2x+5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186291685}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "f98dd494-7f2f-57dc-af90-42867eb57511" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{x}^{2}-2x+5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186291685} :answer-field/id #uuid "c5c7a55c-b5a8-5ece-9653-1e4addb15710" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186292338}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "adc18662-35c2-5630-b6c2-775bfa727c41" :question/math-academy-id "q-276277" :question/problem "Consider the curve defined parametrically as\n\n$$\nx=t+1,\\,\\,\\,y={t}^{2}+4,\\,t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).\n$$\n\nThe Cartesian equation of this curve is\n\n$y=$\n{{field-1}}." :question/worked-solution "To find the Cartesian equation, we eliminate $t.$ First, we use the $x$ equation to solve for $t:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x=t+1\\,⇒\\,t=x-1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, substituting $t=x-1$ into our expression for $y,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =(x-1)^{2}+4 \\\\ y & ={x}^{2}-2x+5\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the Cartesian equation of the given curve is \n$y={x}^{2}-2x+5.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186292918 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "efebc768-607d-542f-b7b0-0c55ce311093" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{2}{9}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186291635}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "efebc768-607d-542f-b7b0-0c55ce311093" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{2}{9}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186291635} :answer-field/id #uuid "49ec9008-738f-5560-b1c0-bab64077c3f0" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186291970} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "27cbd4cc-c040-570c-8981-361a2c723b70" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{4x+1}{9x+2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186290796}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "27cbd4cc-c040-570c-8981-361a2c723b70" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{4x+1}{9x+2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186290796} :answer-field/id #uuid "cd2e0a7d-9fb9-581d-bf54-ae428568db6f" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186292355}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "3e3af460-d835-5ff0-b7d7-10fb0256d9da" :question/math-academy-id "q-276284" :question/problem "Consider the curve defined parametrically as\n\n$$\nx=\\frac{1}{t-4},\\,\\,\\,y=\\frac{t}{2t+1},\\,t\\ne 4,\\,\\,\\,t\\ne -\\frac{1}{2}.\n$$\n\nThe Cartesian equation of this curve is\n\n$y=$\n{{field-1}}$,\\,x\\ne $\n{{field-2}}$,\\,\\,\\,x\\ne 0.$" :question/worked-solution "To find the Cartesian equation, we eliminate $t.$ First, we use the $x$ equation to solve for $t:$\n\n$$\nx=\\frac{1}{t-4}\\,⇒\\,t=\\frac{4x+1}{x}\n$$\n\nNow, substituting $t=\\frac{4x+1}{x}$ into our expression for $y,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =\\frac{(\\frac{4x+1}{x})}{(2\\cdot \\frac{4x+1}{x}+1)} \\\\ y & =\\frac{(\\frac{4x+1}{x})}{(\\frac{8x+2}{x}+1)} \\\\ y & =\\frac{(\\frac{4x+1}{x})}{(\\frac{9x+2}{x})} \\\\ y & =\\frac{4x+1}{\\cancel{x}}\\cdot \\frac{\\cancel{x}}{9x+2} \\\\ y & =\\frac{4x+1}{9x+2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the Cartesian equation of the given curve is $y=\\frac{4x+1}{9x+2}.$\n\nFinally, since $t\\ne -\\frac{1}{2}$ and $x=\\frac{1}{t-4}$ has range $x\\ne 0,$ we must have \n$x\\ne -\\frac{2}{9},\\text{ }0.$ \n\nTherefore, the complete Cartesian equation is\n\n$$\ny=\\frac{4x+1}{9x+2},\\,x\\ne -\\frac{2}{9},\\,\\,\\,x\\ne 0.\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186299913 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1a1c86cb-411d-534e-810a-79bce25d9b55" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{x}^{2}+4x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186297663}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "1a1c86cb-411d-534e-810a-79bce25d9b55" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{x}^{2}+4x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186297663} :answer-field/id #uuid "2c103235-e1c7-50cf-97f5-b61725f0ce9e" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186298789}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "0c26af88-186d-5e0b-b417-96d3412f3929" :question/math-academy-id "q-320871" :question/problem "Consider the curve defined parametrically as\n\n$$\nx=t-2,\\,\\,\\,y={t}^{2}-4,\\,t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).\n$$\n\nThe Cartesian equation of this curve is\n\n$y=$\n{{field-1}}." :question/worked-solution "To find the Cartesian equation, we eliminate $t.$ First, we use the $x$ equation to solve for $t:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x=t-2\\,⇒\\,t=x+2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, substituting $t=x+2$ into our expression for $y,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =(x+2)^{2}-4 \\\\ y & ={x}^{2}+4x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the Cartesian equation of the given curve is \n$y={x}^{2}+4x.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186299919 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "cd8f4c87-04b9-5012-91a0-2fa1e73539ce" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1-3x}{7x-2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186298449}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "cd8f4c87-04b9-5012-91a0-2fa1e73539ce" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1-3x}{7x-2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186298449} :answer-field/id #uuid "d10035ab-a533-5291-9723-0bc6c8afa230" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186299345} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "5584afb0-f812-5610-86a2-9366973652b3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{7}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186297913}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "5584afb0-f812-5610-86a2-9366973652b3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{7}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186297913} :answer-field/id #uuid "d2071233-48c1-53b8-9e87-ce0c7ce6bb94" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186299350}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "80110151-a320-56a4-96e5-9888e94599a9" :question/math-academy-id "q-320926" :question/problem "Consider the curve defined parametrically as\n\n$$\nx=\\frac{1}{t+3},\\,\\,\\,y=\\frac{t}{-2t+1},\\,t\\ne -3,\\,\\,\\,t\\ne \\frac{1}{2}.\n$$\n\nThe Cartesian equation of this curve is\n\n$y=$\n{{field-1}}$,\\,x\\ne $\n{{field-2}}$,\\,\\,\\,x\\ne 0.$" :question/worked-solution "To find the Cartesian equation, we eliminate $t.$ First, we use the $x$ equation to solve for $t:$\n\n$$\nx=\\frac{1}{t+3}\\,⇒\\,t=-3+\\frac{1}{x}\n$$\n\nNow, substituting $t=-3+\\frac{1}{x}$ into our expression for $y,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =\\frac{(-3+\\frac{1}{x})}{(-2\\,(-3+\\frac{1}{x})+1)} \\\\  & =\\frac{(-3+\\frac{1}{x})}{(6-\\frac{2}{x}+1)} \\\\  & =\\frac{(-3+\\frac{1}{x})}{(7-\\frac{2}{x})} \\\\  & =\\frac{-3x+1}{\\cancel{x}}\\cdot \\frac{\\cancel{x}}{7x-2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1-3x}{7x-2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the Cartesian equation of the given curve is $y=\\frac{1-3x}{7x-2}.$\n\nFinally, since $t\\ne \\frac{1}{2}$ and $x=\\frac{1}{t+3}$ has range $x\\ne 0,$ we must have \n$x\\ne \\frac{2}{7},\\text{ }0.$ \n\nTherefore, the complete Cartesian equation is\n\n$$\ny=\\frac{1-3x}{7x-2},\\,x\\ne \\frac{2}{7},\\,\\,\\,x\\ne 0.\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186299923 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "52d81c8c-e7c3-5d18-9722-ffa9b4fbde0b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3(3x+1)(x-1)(2-3x)^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186297902}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "52d81c8c-e7c3-5d18-9722-ffa9b4fbde0b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3(3x+1)(x-1)(2-3x)^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186297902} :answer-field/id #uuid "a3f57b3d-a654-5285-ac31-3e8cdc988821" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186299186}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "49a7b1ee-edb5-5cfe-b0e7-c77c9612e0b8" :question/math-academy-id "q-321074" :question/problem "Find the Cartesian equation of the curve defined parametrically by\n\n$$\nx=\\frac{t+1}{3},\\,\\,\\,y=({t}^{2}-4)(1-t)^{2},\\,t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).\n$$\n\n$y=$\n{{field-1}}." :question/worked-solution "To find the Cartesian equation, we eliminate $t.$ First, we use the $x$ equation to solve for $t:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x=\\frac{t+1}{3}\\,⇒\\,t=3x-1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, substituting $t=3x-1$ into our expression for $y,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =((3x-1)^{2}-4){(1-(3x-1))}^{2} \\\\  & =(9{x}^{2}-6x+1-4)(2-3x)^{2} \\\\  & =(9{x}^{2}-6x-3)(2-3x)^{2} \\\\  & =3(3{x}^{2}-2x-1)(2-3x)^{2} \\\\  & =3(3x+1)(x-1)(2-3x)^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the Cartesian equation of the given curve is $y=3(3x+1)(x-1)(2-3x)^{2}.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186299924 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1e6c8835-3261-554a-afbb-957028b9b565" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "32x(2x-1)^{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186297684}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "1e6c8835-3261-554a-afbb-957028b9b565" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "32x(2x-1)^{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186297684} :answer-field/id #uuid "f4679020-39ab-52d5-99d1-edaaf51fac68" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186299460}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "4084c354-7618-56fc-b268-f7034497a330" :question/math-academy-id "q-321075" :question/problem "Find the Cartesian equation of the curve defined parametrically by\n\n$$\nx=\\frac{1-t}{4},\\,\\,\\,y=({t}^{2}-1)(1+t)^{2},\\,t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).\n$$\n\n$y=$\n{{field-1}}." :question/worked-solution "To find the Cartesian equation, we eliminate $t.$ First, we use the $x$ equation to solve for $t:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x=\\frac{1-t}{4}\\,⇒\\,t=1-4x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, substituting $t=1-4x$ into our expression for $y,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =((1-4x)^{2}-1)((1+(1-4x))^{2}) \\\\  & =(1-8x+16{x}^{2}-1)((2-4x)^{2}) \\\\  & =(-8x+16{x}^{2})(4)(1-2x)^{2} \\\\  & =8x(2x-1)(2-4x)^{2} \\\\  & =8x(2x-1)\\cdot 4(1-2x)^{2} \\\\  & =32x(2x-1)^{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the Cartesian equation of the given curve is $y=32x(2x-1)^{3}.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186299925 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "f5593662-8d5b-547d-8bc2-6520dd120c28" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "({x}^{2}-1)(5-{x}^{2})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186298596}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "f5593662-8d5b-547d-8bc2-6520dd120c28" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "({x}^{2}-1)(5-{x}^{2})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186298596} :answer-field/id #uuid "baa6ee25-0549-5c84-9148-4b6a5918be3e" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186299270}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "78ac25c2-7c10-5369-8ba8-e6189caf0a34" :question/math-academy-id "q-321084" :question/problem "Consider the curve defined parametrically as\n\n$$\nx=\\sqrt{\\,t+1\\,},\\,\\,\\,y=t(4-t),\\,t\\ge 0.\n$$\n\nThe Cartesian equation of this curve is\n\n$y=$\n{{field-1}}$,\\,x\\ge 1.$" :question/worked-solution "To find the Cartesian equation, we eliminate $t.$ First, we use the $x$ equation to solve for $t:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x=\\sqrt{\\,t+1\\,}\\,⇒\\,t={x}^{2}-1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, substituting $t={x}^{2}-1$ into our expression for $y,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =({x}^{2}-1)(4-({x}^{2}-1)) \\\\  & =({x}^{2}-1)(5-{x}^{2})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the Cartesian equation of the given curve is $y=({x}^{2}-1)(5-{x}^{2}).$\n\nFinally, since $t\\ge 0$ and $t={x}^{2}-1,$ we must have ${x}^{2}-1\\ge 0,$ i.e., $x\\ge 1.$\n\nTherefore, the complete Cartesian equation is $y=({x}^{2}-1)(5-{x}^{2}),\\,x\\ge 1.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186299926 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "d744dcd4-0a3e-511e-b60c-7b2673712e59" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3{x}^{2}+27" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186298478}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "d744dcd4-0a3e-511e-b60c-7b2673712e59" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3{x}^{2}+27" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186298478} :answer-field/id #uuid "8387e755-e4a8-5276-9d88-37aa96b73bfd" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186299081}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "7fac9ad7-3b23-560c-8033-d32b15e29ec7" :question/math-academy-id "q-321090" :question/problem "Consider the curve defined parametrically as\n\n$$\nx=\\sqrt{{t}^{2}-9},\\,\\,\\,y=3{t}^{2},\\,t\\ge 3.\n$$\n\nThe Cartesian equation of this curve is\n\n$y=$\n{{field-1}}$,\\,x\\ge 0.$" :question/worked-solution "To find the Cartesian equation, we eliminate $t.$ First, we use the $x$ equation to solve for ${t}^{2}:$\n\n$$\nx=\\sqrt{{t}^{2}-9}\\,⇒\\,{t}^{2}={x}^{2}+9\n$$\n\nNow, substituting ${t}^{2}={x}^{2}+9$ into our expression for $y,$ we get\n\n$$\ny=3({x}^{2}+9)=3{x}^{2}+27.\n$$\n\nSo, the Cartesian equation of the given curve is $y=3{x}^{2}+27.$\n\nFinally, since $t\\ge 3$ and $x=\\sqrt{{t}^{2}-9},$ we must have $x\\ge 0.$\n\nTherefore, the complete Cartesian equation is $y=3{x}^{2}+27,\\,x\\ge 0.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186299929 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "d45792f3-0a28-56c8-a67d-3d50eaf93eb2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186298464}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "d45792f3-0a28-56c8-a67d-3d50eaf93eb2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186298464} :answer-field/id #uuid "1b761f73-9699-5009-890b-c604bb787aa6" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186298735} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "fba2eb2c-542e-5741-9da4-b68fe7825059" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{(1+x)^{2}}{4{x}^{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186298625}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "fba2eb2c-542e-5741-9da4-b68fe7825059" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{(1+x)^{2}}{4{x}^{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186298625} :answer-field/id #uuid "eb33573b-f770-5250-8223-d49dca5de7e1" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186299433}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "ce51648f-7c56-5fd1-a391-8983dd27aa88" :question/math-academy-id "q-321141" :question/problem "Consider the curve defined parametrically as\n\n$$\nx=\\frac{1}{2t+1},\\,\\,\\,y=(t+1)^{2},\\,t\\ne -\\frac{1}{2}.\n$$\n\nThe Cartesian equation of this curve is\n\n$y=$\n{{field-1}}$,\\,x\\ne $\n{{field-2}}." :question/worked-solution "To find the Cartesian equation, we eliminate $t.$ First, we use the $x$ equation to solve for $t:$\n\n$$\nx=\\frac{1}{2t+1}\\,⇒\\,2t+1=\\frac{1}{x}\\,⇒\\,t=\\frac{1}{2x}-\\frac{1}{2}.\n$$\n\nNow, substituting $t=\\frac{1}{2x}-\\frac{1}{2}$ into our expression for $y,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & ={(\\frac{1}{2x}-\\frac{1}{2}+1)}^{2} \\\\  & ={(\\frac{1}{2x}+\\frac{1}{2})}^{2} \\\\  & ={(\\frac{1+x}{2x})}^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{(1+x)^{2}}{4{x}^{2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the Cartesian equation of the given curve is $y=\\frac{(1+x)^{2}}{4{x}^{2}}.$\n\nFinally, since $t\\ne -\\frac{1}{2}$ and $x=\\frac{1}{2t+1}$ we must have $x\\ne 0.$\n\nTherefore, the complete Cartesian equation is\n\n$$\ny=\\frac{(1+x)^{2}}{4{x}^{2}},\\,x\\ne 0.\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186302062 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "b3625268-1369-5f41-a534-a0649eceb53b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186300856}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "b3625268-1369-5f41-a534-a0649eceb53b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186300856} :answer-field/id #uuid "20d409ef-248e-5eab-a01b-2a42296f4315" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186301330} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "e2d9df89-b63c-55a8-bd14-62b985a73272" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{x-2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186301091}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "e2d9df89-b63c-55a8-bd14-62b985a73272" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{x-2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186301091} :answer-field/id #uuid "e4a0ae5c-3a22-5796-9f64-eda5a7edb186" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186301951}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "6b94a700-bd85-58aa-bc0c-7585b2834748" :question/math-academy-id "q-321410" :question/problem "Consider the curve defined parametrically as\n\n$$\nx=-3t+2,\\,\\,\\,y=-\\frac{1}{t},\\,t\\ne 0.\n$$\n\nThe Cartesian equation of this curve is\n\n$y=$\n{{field-1}}$,\\,x\\ne $\n{{field-2}}." :question/worked-solution "To find the Cartesian equation, we eliminate $t.$ First, we use the $x$ equation to solve for $t:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x=-3t+2\\,⇒\\,t=\\frac{2-x}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, substituting $t=\\frac{2-x}{3}$ into our expression for $y,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =-\\frac{1}{(\\frac{2-x}{3})} \\\\ y & =\\frac{3}{x-2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the Cartesian equation of the given curve is $y=\\frac{3}{x-2}.$\n\nFinally, since $t\\ne 0$ and $x=-3t+2$ , we must have $x\\ne 2.$ \n\nTherefore, the complete Cartesian equation is\n\n$$\ny=\\frac{3}{x-2},\\,x\\ne 2.\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186335960 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "085c2edd-3369-5dc5-a8be-7a04e4833c75" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=\\frac{1}{x-5},\\,x\\ne 5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186333070} {:answer/id #uuid "23ef77a2-3fdc-5a4c-8d16-fa9808fc0f18" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=-\\frac{2}{x},\\,x\\ne 0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186333321} {:answer/id #uuid "392acd54-3425-5339-9d3d-6a019cf5800e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}},\\,x\\ne 0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186333524} {:answer/id #uuid "99b89343-add4-5f68-b419-80457c1c4731" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=\\frac{5}{x},\\,x\\ne 0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186334347} {:answer/id #uuid "f75b4892-5861-5baf-acfb-fc468d5720ec" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=\\frac{1}{x+5},\\,x\\ne -5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186335156}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "085c2edd-3369-5dc5-a8be-7a04e4833c75" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=\\frac{1}{x-5},\\,x\\ne 5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186333070} :answer-field/id #uuid "406437c4-819d-5269-8f4a-49f5f3df3622" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186335362}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "0026543b-fef7-5b9b-a515-31dc0ad33f34" :question/math-academy-id "q-6828" :question/problem "Find the Cartesian equation of the curve defined parametrically by\n\n$$\nx={t}^{2}+5,\\,\\,\\,y=\\frac{1}{{t}^{2}},\\,t\\ne 0.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "To find the Cartesian equation, we eliminate $t.$ First, we use the $x$ equation to solve for ${t}^{2}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x={t}^{2}+5\\,⇒\\,{t}^{2}=x-5\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, substituting ${t}^{2}=x-5$ into our expression for $y,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =\\frac{1}{x-5}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo the Cartesian equation of the given curve is $y=\\frac{1}{x-5}.$\n\nFinally, since $t\\ne 0$ and $x={t}^{2}+5,$ we must have $x\\ne 5.$ \n\nSo, the complete Cartesian equation is\n\n$$\ny=\\frac{1}{x-5},\\,x\\ne 5.\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186335961 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "63c62779-8b0d-585a-b920-9d1b9d20ae31" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=2{x}^{2}+8,\\,x\\ge 0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186333894} {:answer/id #uuid "9e017776-ba43-519f-8ae0-9da1f2fddf1f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=4{x}^{2}+8,\\,x\\ge 0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186334380} {:answer/id #uuid "bf39d551-3f09-580c-8c58-bb70cec2a129" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=4{x}^{2}-16,\\,x\\ge 0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186334648} {:answer/id #uuid "e494c19c-979d-5647-9d5a-9c8e8a25d8e0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=2{x}^{2}+4,\\,x\\ge 0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186334982} {:answer/id #uuid "f3188548-f10a-5f7e-b17b-d07a087d2bfb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=2{x}^{2}-8,\\,x\\ge 0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186335119}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "63c62779-8b0d-585a-b920-9d1b9d20ae31" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=2{x}^{2}+8,\\,x\\ge 0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186333894} :answer-field/id #uuid "dfad0156-2e42-5fec-aefe-931c3016a260" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186335630}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "22819da8-eeba-5d43-b779-66f241379e67" :question/math-academy-id "q-6830" :question/problem "Find the Cartesian equation of the curve defined parametrically by\n\n$$\nx=\\sqrt{{t}^{2}-4},\\,\\,\\,y=2{t}^{2},\\,t\\ge 2.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "To find the Cartesian equation, we eliminate $t.$ First, we use the $x$ equation to solve for ${t}^{2}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x=\\sqrt{{t}^{2}-4}\\,⇒\\,{t}^{2}={x}^{2}+4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, substituting ${t}^{2}={x}^{2}+4$ into our expression for $y,$ we get\n\n$$\ny=2({x}^{2}+4)=2{x}^{2}+8\n$$\n\nSo the Cartesian equation of the given curve is $y=2{x}^{2}+8.$\n\nFinally, since $t\\ge 2.$ and $x=\\sqrt{{t}^{2}-4},$ we must have $x\\ge 0.$\n\nSo, the complete Cartesian equation is\n\n$$\ny=2{x}^{2}+8,\\,x\\ge 0.\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186377040 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "33526b37-cab0-5457-9e07-446f0c05aa08" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{4}(x-9)(x-3)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186377042}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "33526b37-cab0-5457-9e07-446f0c05aa08" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{4}(x-9)(x-3)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186377042} :answer-field/id #uuid "e36531d7-495c-5197-b612-e0e22cd3b3b5" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186377041}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "22eb775d-0ea8-57a9-a014-0d8618741de6" :question/math-academy-id "q-276278" :question/problem "Consider the curve defined parametrically as\n\n$$\nx=2t+5,\\,\\,\\,y=(t-2)(t+1),\\,t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).\n$$\n\nThe Cartesian equation of this curve is\n\n$y=$\n{{field-1}}." :question/worked-solution "To find the Cartesian equation, we eliminate $t.$ First, we use the $x$ equation to solve for $t:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x=2t+5\\,⇒\\,t=\\frac{x-5}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, substituting $t=\\frac{x-5}{2}$ into our expression for $y,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =(\\frac{x-5}{2}-2)(\\frac{x-5}{2}+1) \\\\  & =(\\frac{x-5-4}{2})(\\frac{x-5+2}{2}) \\\\  & =(\\frac{x-9}{2})(\\frac{x-3}{2}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}(x-9)(x-3).\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186377085 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "e76ec15d-4235-56b9-b033-fb60f2aa5631" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{9}(x+16)(x-5)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186377087}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "e76ec15d-4235-56b9-b033-fb60f2aa5631" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{9}(x+16)(x-5)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186377087} :answer-field/id #uuid "d4e1d208-e4d2-5915-83c1-b4b15806c88a" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186377086}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "081bb8fa-7465-5289-83b2-398e18ea3051" :question/math-academy-id "q-320878" :question/problem "Consider the curve defined parametrically as\n\n$$\nx=3t-1,\\,\\,\\,y=(t+5)(t-2),\\,t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).\n$$\n\nThe Cartesian equation of this curve is\n\n$y=$\n{{field-1}}." :question/worked-solution "To find the Cartesian equation, we eliminate $t.$ First, we use the $x$ equation to solve for $t:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x=3t-1\\,⇒\\,t=\\frac{x+1}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, substituting $t=\\frac{x+1}{3}$ into our expression for $y,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =(\\frac{x+1}{3}+5)(\\frac{x+1}{3}-2) \\\\  & =(\\frac{x+1+15}{3})(\\frac{x+1-6}{3}) \\\\  & =(\\frac{x+16}{3})(\\frac{x-5}{3}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{9}(x+16)(x-5).\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Cartesian Equation of a Curve Defined Parametrically"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186100893 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186100728 :question/id #uuid "dbb4e0a0-55ec-5cc7-9617-99cec8108940" :question/math-academy-id "e-540" :question/problem "Find the particular solution to the differential equation $y' = 5y$ that satisfies $y(0)=3.$" :question/worked-solution "We first find the general solution, and then apply the given initial condition to find a particular solution.\n\nSeparating variables and integrating both sides with respect to $x,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =5y \\\\ \\frac{1}{y}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =5 \\\\ \\int \\frac{1}{y}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}\\,\\text{d}x & =5\\int \\,\\text{d}x \\\\ \\int \\frac{1}{y}\\,\\text{d}y & =5\\int \\,\\text{d}x \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡|y| & =5x+C\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nApplying the exponential function to both sides, we find the general solution:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =\\pm {e}^{5x+C} \\\\  & =\\pm {e}^{C}{e}^{5x} \\\\  & =K{e}^{5x}\\,,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $K=\\pm e^{C}$ is an arbitrary constant. \n\nNow we apply the condition $y(0) = 3$ to find $K\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y(0) & =3 \\\\ K{e}^{5\\cdot 0} & =3 \\\\ K & =3\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus the particular solution of $y' = 5y$ that satisfies the condition $y(0)=3$ is\n\n$$\ny = 3 e^{5x}  \\,.\n$$"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "99112850-043d-53e5-8d61-fecb0317bd11" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048697 :topic/math-academy-id 466 :topic/title "Solving First-Order ODEs Using Separation of Variables"}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186101401 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "446c4857-dd0c-5efd-afb0-2ea4ed0660a0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{y}^{3}=\\frac{1}{3}{x}^{2}-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186324526} {:answer/id #uuid "748ec2ec-3297-5615-86ab-bc1f21ac26f1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{y}^{3}={x}^{2}+\\frac{1}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186324942} {:answer/id #uuid "9c45be7d-d1cc-59a4-8002-8c72c910bd2c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{y}^{3}=3{x}^{2}+1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186325297} {:answer/id #uuid "d5becbca-f53a-5866-bb37-73887a660a89" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{y}^{3}=\\frac{1}{6}{x}^{2}+1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186325829} {:answer/id #uuid "e7e878f5-c14b-5673-a208-7eea879de351" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{y}^{3}={x}^{2}+1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186326013}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "9c45be7d-d1cc-59a4-8002-8c72c910bd2c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{y}^{3}=3{x}^{2}+1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186325297} :answer-field/id #uuid "d6850fb7-3a0b-5d41-af17-c643ce765bf0" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186326610}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "6c894685-37ff-5d18-b9c8-6edb0d037176" :question/math-academy-id "q-4019" :question/problem "Find the particular solution to the differential equation $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{2x}{{y}^{2}}$ that satisfies $y(2)=\\sqrt[3]{13}.$" :question/worked-solution "We first find the general solution and then apply the given initial condition to find the particular solution.\n\nSeparating variables and integrating both sides with respect to $x,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{2x}{{y}^{2}} \\\\ {y}^{2}\\,\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =2x \\\\ \\int {y}^{2}\\,\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int 2x\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ \\int {y}^{2}\\,\\text{d}y & =\\int 2x\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ \\frac{{y}^{3}}{3} & ={x}^{2}+{C}_{1} \\\\ {y}^{3} & =3{x}^{2}+3{C}_{1}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe conclude that the general solution is\n\n$$\n{y}^{3}=3{x}^{2}+C,\n$$\n\nwhere $C=3{C}_{1}$  is an arbitrary constant.\n\nWe now apply the condition $y(2)=\\sqrt[3]{13}$ to find $C:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{(\\sqrt[3]{13})}^{3} & =3(2)^{2}+C \\\\ 13 & =12+C \\\\ C & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, the particular solution of $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{2x}{{y}^{2}}$ that satisfies the condition $y(2)=\\sqrt[3]{13}$ is\n\n$$\n{y}^{3}=3{x}^{2}+1.\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186101545 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "531c9876-41a6-5528-8716-512d9860a650" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=\\frac{2}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡x-1}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186349207} {:answer/id #uuid "63de5349-a542-5177-bb56-e3b3bd658803" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=\\frac{2}{1-2\\operatorname{ln}⁡x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186349360} {:answer/id #uuid "86d94f8e-237b-5f46-bbef-54ff8f75c5cc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=\\frac{2}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡x+1}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186349675} {:answer/id #uuid "89e3ffe6-f402-5afd-82f1-aa385f26d411" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=\\frac{2}{2\\operatorname{ln}⁡x+1}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186349698} {:answer/id #uuid "d835efde-95ab-5abd-b768-7494d10b73eb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=\\frac{2}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡x-2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186350340}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "63de5349-a542-5177-bb56-e3b3bd658803" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=\\frac{2}{1-2\\operatorname{ln}⁡x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186349360} :answer-field/id #uuid "375659ba-c2ed-5aba-9b69-1aebcdb68de1" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186350802}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "525ed303-6429-5295-90a0-f24212406b6d" :question/math-academy-id "q-8310" :question/problem "Find the particular solution to the differential equation $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{{y}^{2}}{x}$ that satisfies $y(1)=2.$ Assume that $x>0.$" :question/worked-solution "We first find the general solution and then apply the given initial condition to find the particular solution.\n\nSeparating variables and integrating both sides with respect to $x,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{{y}^{2}}{x} \\\\ \\frac{1}{{y}^{2}}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{1}{x} \\\\ \\int \\frac{1}{{y}^{2}}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int \\frac{1}{x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ \\int \\frac{1}{{y}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}y & =\\int \\frac{1}{x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ -\\frac{1}{y} & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x|+C \\\\ \\frac{1}{y} & =-(\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x|+C) \\\\ y & =-\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x|+C}\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAssuming $x>0,$ the general solution is\n\n$$\ny=-\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡x+C},\n$$\n\nwhere $C$ is an arbitrary constant.\n\nWe now apply the condition $y(1)=2$ to find $C:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y(1) & =2 \\\\ -\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡1+C} & =2 \\\\ -\\frac{1}{C} & =2 \\\\ C & =-\\frac{1}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, the particular solution of $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{{y}^{2}}{x}$ that satisfies the condition $y(1)=2$ is\n\n$$\ny=\\frac{-1}{(\\operatorname{ln}⁡x-\\frac{1}{2})}=\\frac{2}{1-2\\operatorname{ln}⁡x}\\,.\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186235520 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "b45fa11b-7aeb-5dbf-a888-002c910fdf5e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "25+3{e}^{-0.03t}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186234823}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "b45fa11b-7aeb-5dbf-a888-002c910fdf5e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "25+3{e}^{-0.03t}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186234823} :answer-field/id #uuid "ba2c9880-594d-5761-a31f-7e56aaee7f89" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186235200}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "cdb03268-a0ae-53d3-84de-7af60bc110d4" :question/math-academy-id "q-168078" :question/problem "Let $\\overset{^}{T}(t)$ be a particular solution of the differential equation from the previous part that satisfies the condition $\\overset{^}{T}(0)=28.$ Find $\\overset{^}{T}(t).$\n\n                            \n                                    \n                                        \n                                            \n\n                                                \n\n                                        \n                                        \n                                        \n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        $\\overset{^}{T}(t)\\,=\\,$\n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                            \n                                        {{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "Previously, we found that the general solution is\n\n$$\nT=25+K{e}^{-0.03t}.\n$$\n\nwhere $K$ is an arbitrary constant.\n\nWe now apply the condition $T(0)=28$ to find $K:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}28 & =25+K\\,{e}^{-0.03(0)} \\\\ 28 & =25+K\\,{e}^{0} \\\\ K & =3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, the required particular solution is\n\n$$\n\\overset{^}{T}(t)=25+3{e}^{-0.03t}.\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186240789 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2402a50b-0ada-5a95-bd1c-d60ffe3a7054" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{cos}⁡2y=-2x+1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186238371} {:answer/id #uuid "3113425a-30f8-5c8b-88b3-34e913ccf225" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{sin}⁡y=-2x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186238481} {:answer/id #uuid "79eaa63a-7157-5816-ab3b-72d58acb2790" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{sin}⁡y=x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186239106} {:answer/id #uuid "f7406111-8e32-5b08-aa41-0f61204a9031" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{cos}⁡y=x+1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186240235} {:answer/id #uuid "fd71d029-d6be-50c5-9b70-7d320b3399c3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{cos}⁡y=-x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186240297}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "fd71d029-d6be-50c5-9b70-7d320b3399c3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{cos}⁡y=-x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186240297} :answer-field/id #uuid "d1159eaa-cd25-57b9-b6f3-0999eae2bb5f" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186240700}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "1086559f-da78-5256-be5a-8d4d9777777a" :question/math-academy-id "q-104055" :question/problem "Find the particular solution to the differential equation $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡y}$ that satisfies $y(0)=\\frac{\\pi }{2}.$" :question/worked-solution "We first find the general solution and then apply the given initial condition to find the particular solution.\n\nSeparating variables and integrating both sides with respect to $x,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡y} \\\\ \\operatorname{sin}⁡y\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =1 \\\\ \\int \\operatorname{sin}⁡y\\,\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int \\,\\text{d}x \\\\ \\int \\operatorname{sin}⁡y\\,\\text{d}y & =\\int \\,\\text{d}x \\\\ -\\operatorname{cos}⁡y & =x+C \\\\ \\operatorname{cos}⁡y & =-x-C.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe now apply the condition $y(0)=\\frac{\\pi }{2}$ to find $C:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{2}) & =-0-C \\\\ 0 & =0-C \\\\ C & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo we get the particular solution in implicit form:\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{cos}⁡y=-x\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186275103 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "01b271c8-930d-55d2-b6c9-4d10415d1f40" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=\\operatorname{tan}⁡(x-\\frac{\\pi }{4})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186272398} {:answer/id #uuid "453026c0-09cc-5849-a51c-b0765a6a347c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=\\operatorname{cot}⁡(x-\\frac{\\pi }{4})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186272980} {:answer/id #uuid "7f4bfc4f-7d7a-5012-b1f2-ad8906f9fa0d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=\\operatorname{tan}⁡(x+\\frac{\\pi }{4})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186273481} {:answer/id #uuid "a9c5c442-efee-51ea-b4c4-619423884639" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=\\operatorname{tan}⁡(x+\\frac{\\pi }{2})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186273854} {:answer/id #uuid "dcd30bde-3736-5aa2-a5e8-1c3be5c7c83c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=\\operatorname{cot}⁡(x+\\frac{\\pi }{2})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186274328}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "01b271c8-930d-55d2-b6c9-4d10415d1f40" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=\\operatorname{tan}⁡(x-\\frac{\\pi }{4})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186272398} :answer-field/id #uuid "6bfbd7fa-8ea2-5c3c-b15b-9c490e6cfc9b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186274817}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "c2219a64-4fca-5417-a4ef-4988c9db8d90" :question/math-academy-id "q-14310" :question/problem "Find the specific solution to the differential equation $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}={y}^{2}+1$ given that  $y(\\frac{\\pi }{2})=1.$" :question/worked-solution "We first find the general solution and then apply the given initial condition to find the particular solution.\n\nSeparating variables and integrating both sides with respect to $x,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}={y}^{2}+1 \\\\ \\frac{1}{{y}^{2}+1}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =1 \\\\ \\int \\frac{1}{{y}^{2}+1}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int \\,\\text{d}x \\\\ \\int \\frac{1}{{y}^{2}+1}\\,\\text{d}y & =\\int \\,\\text{d}x \\\\ \\operatorname{arctan}⁡(y) & =x+C \\\\ y & =\\operatorname{tan}⁡(x+C).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe conclude that the general solution is\n\n$$\ny=\\operatorname{tan}⁡(x+C).\n$$\n\nWe now apply the condition $y(\\frac{\\pi }{2})=1$ to find $C$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}1 & =\\operatorname{tan}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{2}+C) \\\\ \\operatorname{arctan}⁡(1) & =\\frac{\\pi }{2}+C \\\\ \\frac{\\pi }{4} & =\\frac{\\pi }{2}+C \\\\ C & =\\frac{\\pi }{4}-\\frac{\\pi }{2} \\\\ C & =-\\frac{\\pi }{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, the particular solution of $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}={y}^{2}+1$ that satisfies the condition $y(\\frac{\\pi }{2})=1$ is\n\n$$\ny=\\operatorname{tan}⁡(x-\\frac{\\pi }{4}).\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186275104 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "161701b8-cab3-5a52-baee-ca387468d340" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y={e}^{-1+x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186272583} {:answer/id #uuid "50b36c20-41be-54bd-8252-8418beace6e0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y={e}^{-{e}^{-x}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186273071} {:answer/id #uuid "6737182b-f42e-57d4-b8d6-67a37fb50f8d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y={e}^{{e}^{x}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186273273} {:answer/id #uuid "7771e672-13a1-5981-b9ae-117088631933" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y={e}^{-1-{e}^{x}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186273404} {:answer/id #uuid "db69fd4d-8719-5651-b5ad-029bab24e359" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y={e}^{-1+{e}^{-x}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186274311}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "50b36c20-41be-54bd-8252-8418beace6e0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y={e}^{-{e}^{-x}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186273071} :answer-field/id #uuid "62ccf029-8cdb-5f2e-b996-01a4b881873e" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186274806}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "dd2fa0cf-e14d-5280-8a92-ce27abb0aa11" :question/math-academy-id "q-14312" :question/problem "Find the particular solution to the differential equation $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=y{e}^{-x}$ that satisfies $y(0)={e}^{-1}.$" :question/worked-solution "We first find the general solution and then apply the given initial condition to find the particular solution.\n\nSeparating variables and integrating both sides with respect to $x,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =y{e}^{-x} \\\\ \\frac{1}{y}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & ={e}^{-x} \\\\ \\int \\frac{1}{y}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}\\,dx & =\\int {e}^{-x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ \\int \\frac{1}{y}\\,\\text{d}y & =\\int {e}^{-x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡|y| & =-{e}^{-x}+C.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nApplying the exponential function to both sides, we find the general solution:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{e}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡|y|} & ={e}^{-{e}^{-x}+C} \\\\ |y| & ={e}^{-{e}^{-x}+C} \\\\ y & =\\pm {e}^{-{e}^{-x}+C} \\\\ y & =K{e}^{-{e}^{-x}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $K=\\pm {e}^{C}$ is an arbitrary constant. \n\nWe now apply the condition $y(0)={e}^{-1}$ to find $K:$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{e}^{-1} & =K{e}^{-{e}^{-0}} \\\\ {e}^{-1} & =K{e}^{-1} \\\\ K & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, the particular solution of $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=y{e}^{-x}$ that satisfies the condition $y(0)={e}^{-1}$ is\n\n$$\ny={e}^{-{e}^{-x}}.\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186275105 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "184695d3-9029-5e71-b2c0-44b31db02e5d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{y}^{3}=3{x}^{2}+5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186272598} {:answer/id #uuid "1bb941ed-69f9-51b5-84ab-9b185febcd77" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{y}^{3}={x}^{2}+3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186272625} {:answer/id #uuid "664df1fa-7210-55f4-b6c5-5c6c644b8caa" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{y}^{3}={x}^{3}+7" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186273263} {:answer/id #uuid "7bb836b0-ad07-50fc-aaa1-78966b0fe6f2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{y}^{3}=5{x}^{3}+3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186273448} {:answer/id #uuid "93347f2d-2098-56c3-8973-3c2341f180fb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{y}^{3}=2{x}^{3}+2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186273667}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "664df1fa-7210-55f4-b6c5-5c6c644b8caa" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{y}^{3}={x}^{3}+7" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186273263} :answer-field/id #uuid "46fc4299-9139-57f9-a4a0-55185f85b3c0" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186274754}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "c2372277-7745-54d3-839c-0b3553acea76" :question/math-academy-id "q-14313" :question/problem "Find the particular solution to the differential equation $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{{x}^{2}}{{y}^{2}}$  that satisfies $y(1)=2.$" :question/worked-solution "We first find the general solution and then apply the given initial condition to find the particular solution.\n\nSeparating variables and integrating both sides with respect to $x,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{{x}^{2}}{{y}^{2}} \\\\ {y}^{2}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & ={x}^{2} \\\\ \\int {y}^{2}\\,\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int {x}^{2}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ \\int {y}^{2}\\,\\text{d}y & =\\int {x}^{2}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ \\frac{{y}^{3}}{3} & =\\frac{{x}^{3}}{3}+{C}_{1} \\\\ {y}^{3} & ={x}^{3}+C,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere   $C=3{C}_{1}$ is an arbitrary constant.  \n\nWe now apply the condition $y(1)=2$ to find $C:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{2}^{3} & ={1}^{3}+C \\\\ 8-1 & =C \\\\ C & =7\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo we get the particular solution in implicit form:\n\n$$\n{y}^{3}={x}^{3}+7.\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186319204 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "dadf5567-9e34-5988-9b93-ee86c9c672c4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{4x+1}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186318296}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "dadf5567-9e34-5988-9b93-ee86c9c672c4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{4x+1}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186318296} :answer-field/id #uuid "52b5a75f-9489-53f8-a2ef-2d765ab6e701" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186318602}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "0dfecf65-ff44-5a31-9ad0-4e0918756475" :question/math-academy-id "q-347853" :question/problem "Find the particular solution to the differential equation\n$\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+2{y}^{2}=0$\nthat satisfies the condition $y(0)=2.$\n\n$y(x)=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "We first find the general solution and then apply the given initial condition to find the particular solution.\n\nSeparating variables and integrating both sides with respect to $x,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+2{y}^{2} & =0 \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =-2{y}^{2} \\\\ -\\frac{1}{{y}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}y & =2\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ -\\int \\frac{1}{{y}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}y & =\\int 2\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ \\frac{1}{y} & =2x+C \\\\ y & =\\frac{1}{2x+C}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe conclude that the general solution is\n\n$$\ny(x)=\\frac{1}{2x+C},\n$$\n\nwhere $C$ is an arbitrary constant.\n\nWe now apply the condition $y(0)=2$ to find $C:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2 & =\\frac{1}{2(0)+C} \\\\ C & =\\frac{1}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, the particular solution is\n\n$$\ny(x)=\\frac{2}{4x+1}.\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186323684 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a8afe1cc-6a73-59a7-a3aa-86abc400ccd4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{3}{4}\\operatorname{cos}(4x)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322470}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a8afe1cc-6a73-59a7-a3aa-86abc400ccd4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{3}{4}\\operatorname{cos}(4x)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322470} :answer-field/id #uuid "549d1fa9-dbbb-5a28-9ec0-95d21eef029c" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186323329}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "6a3b691d-ce23-5509-8fc2-d490e3df8940" :question/math-academy-id "q-355525" :question/problem "Find the particular solution to the differential equation $\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3y){y}^{′}-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4x)=0$ that satisfies $y(\\frac{\\pi }{8})=0.$ Assume that $-\\frac{\\pi }{6}<y<\\frac{\\pi }{6}.$\n\n$\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3y)=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "We first find the general solution and then apply the given initial condition to find a particular solution.\n\nSeparating variables and integrating both sides with respect to $x,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3y)\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4x) & =0 \\\\ \\operatorname{cos}⁡(3y)\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4x) \\\\ \\operatorname{cos}⁡(3y)\\,\\text{d}y & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ \\int \\operatorname{cos}⁡(3y)\\,\\text{d}y & =\\int \\operatorname{sin}⁡(4x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ \\frac{1}{3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3y) & =-\\frac{1}{4}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4x)+{C}_{1} \\\\ \\operatorname{sin}⁡(3y) & =-\\frac{3}{4}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4x)+C,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $C=3{C}_{1}.$\n\nWe now apply the condition $y(\\frac{\\pi }{8})=0$ to find $C:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y(\\frac{\\pi }{8}) & =0 \\\\ \\operatorname{sin}⁡(3\\cdot 0) & =-\\frac{3}{4}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{8})+C \\\\ 0 & =-\\frac{3}{4}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{2})+C \\\\ 0 & =0+C \\\\ C & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we get the particular solution in implicit form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3y) & =-\\frac{3}{4}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4x)\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186323685 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a897ef12-2c09-5096-a8b5-ee77680d8d9c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "({x}^{2}+2)^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322468}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a897ef12-2c09-5096-a8b5-ee77680d8d9c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "({x}^{2}+2)^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322468} :answer-field/id #uuid "ea786b33-77be-5d8b-adac-7883d2cd2257" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186323631}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "e4bca2e9-3423-5b05-8658-606cf78e9ed4" :question/math-academy-id "q-355528" :question/problem "Find the particular solution to the differential equation\n$\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=4x\\sqrt{y}$\nthat satisfies the condition $y(1)=9.$  Assume that $y>0.$\n\n$y(x)=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "We first find the general solution and then apply the given initial condition to find the particular solution.\n\nSeparating variables and integrating both sides with respect to $x,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =4x\\sqrt{y} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{y}}\\,\\text{d}y & =4x\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ \\int \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{y}}\\,\\text{d}y & =\\int 4x\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ 2\\sqrt{y} & =2{x}^{2}+{C}_{1} \\\\ \\sqrt{y} & ={x}^{2}+\\frac{{C}_{1}}{2} \\\\ y & ={({x}^{2}+\\frac{{C}_{1}}{2})}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAssuming $y>0,$ the general solution is\n\n$$\ny(x)=({x}^{2}+C)^{2},\n$$\n\nwhere $C=\\frac{{C}_{1}}{2}$ is an arbitrary constant.\n\nWe now apply the condition $y(1)=9$ to find $C:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}9 & =((1)^{2}+C)^{2} \\\\ 9 & =(1+C)^{2} \\\\ 3 & =1+C \\\\ C & =2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, the particular solution is\n\n$$\ny(x)=({x}^{2}+2)^{2}.\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186323686 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "96ae4175-e85f-5fa0-9c66-e85a778d8d55" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{4}{7}\\operatorname{tan}(7x)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322350}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "96ae4175-e85f-5fa0-9c66-e85a778d8d55" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{4}{7}\\operatorname{tan}(7x)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322350} :answer-field/id #uuid "aef3a27e-eb31-5e68-9379-c97a63bec369" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186323508}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "64377a61-5efe-5da5-9c93-affa5fd1266f" :question/math-academy-id "q-355544" :question/problem "Find the particular solution to the differential equation ${sec}^{2}⁡(4y){y}^{′}={sec}^{2}⁡(7x)$ that satisfies $y(0)=0.$ Assume that $-\\frac{\\pi }{8}<y<\\frac{\\pi }{8}.$\n\n$\\operatorname{tan}⁡(4y)=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "We first find the general solution and then apply the given initial condition to find a particular solution.\n\nSeparating variables and integrating both sides with respect to $x,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{sec}^{2}⁡(4y)\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & ={sec}^{2}⁡(7x) \\\\ {sec}^{2}⁡(4y)\\,\\text{d}y & ={sec}^{2}⁡(7x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ \\int {sec}^{2}⁡(4y)\\,\\text{d}y & =\\int {sec}^{2}⁡(7x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ \\frac{1}{4}\\operatorname{tan}⁡(4y) & =\\frac{1}{7}\\operatorname{tan}⁡(7x)+{C}_{1} \\\\ \\operatorname{tan}⁡(4y) & =\\frac{4}{7}\\operatorname{tan}⁡(7x)+C,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $C=4{C}_{1}.$\n\nWe now apply the condition $y(0)=0$ to find $C:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y(0) & =0 \\\\ \\operatorname{tan}⁡(4\\cdot 0) & =\\frac{4}{7}\\operatorname{tan}⁡(7\\cdot 0)+C \\\\ 0 & =0+C \\\\ C & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we get the particular solution in implicit form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{tan}⁡(4y) & =\\frac{4}{7}\\operatorname{tan}⁡(7x)\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186323690 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a2e265e1-c3cb-5fab-b4f5-ccf995516d4e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{e}^{{x}^{2}/2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322433}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a2e265e1-c3cb-5fab-b4f5-ccf995516d4e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{e}^{{x}^{2}/2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322433} :answer-field/id #uuid "1b3e560d-f350-5b7b-bd75-6283cbcfd045" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186323221}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "bfb5c5d2-d9d1-5d15-b3d0-8a3479b9922a" :question/math-academy-id "q-355562" :question/problem "Find the particular solution to the differential equation $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=xy$  that satisfies $y(0)=1.$\n\n$y=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "We first find the general solution and then apply the given initial condition to find the particular solution.\n\nSeparating variables and integrating both sides with respect to $x,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =xy \\\\ \\frac{1}{y}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =x \\\\ \\int \\frac{1}{y}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int \\,x\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ \\int \\frac{1}{y}\\,\\text{d}y & =\\int \\,x\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡|y| & =\\frac{1}{2}{x}^{2}+C\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nApplying the exponential function to both sides, we find the general solution:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{e}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡|y|} & ={e}^{{x}^{2}/2+C} \\\\ |y| & ={e}^{{x}^{2}/2+C}\\, \\\\ y & =\\pm {e}^{{x}^{2}/2+C} \\\\ y & =K{e}^{{x}^{2}/2}\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $K=\\pm {e}^{C}$ is an arbitrary constant. \n\nWe now apply the condition $y(0)=1$ to find $K:$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}1 & =K{e}^{0} \\\\ K & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, the particular solution of $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=xy$ that satisfies the condition $y(0)=1$ is\n\n$$\ny={e}^{{x}^{2}/2}\\,.\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186323694 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "ec80c9e4-ade4-588f-871a-59a453ae5b9a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{e}^{2-1/x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186323008}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "ec80c9e4-ade4-588f-871a-59a453ae5b9a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{e}^{2-1/x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186323008} :answer-field/id #uuid "afe21afb-2492-52bc-ac68-6f00ab45aa76" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186323510}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "debef920-e2c6-5181-8631-87e6b6d975ea" :question/math-academy-id "q-355578" :question/problem "Find the particular solution to the differential equation $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}-\\frac{y}{{x}^{2}}=0$  that satisfies $y(1)=e.$\n\n$y=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "We first find the general solution and then apply the given initial condition to find the particular solution.\n\nSeparating variables and integrating both sides with respect to $x,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}-\\frac{y}{{x}^{2}} & =0 \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{y}{{x}^{2}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{y}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}} \\\\ \\int \\frac{1}{y}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int \\,\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ \\int \\frac{1}{y}\\,\\text{d}y & =\\int \\,\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡|y| & =-\\frac{1}{x}+C\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nApplying the exponential function to both sides, we find the general solution:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{e}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡|y|} & ={e}^{-1/x+C} \\\\ |y| & ={e}^{-1/x+C}\\, \\\\ |y| & ={e}^{-1/x}\\cdot {e}^{C} \\\\ y & =K{e}^{-1/x}\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $K=\\pm {e}^{C}$ is an arbitrary constant. \n\nWe now apply the condition $y(1)=e$ to find $K:$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}e & =K{e}^{-1} \\\\ K & ={e}^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, the particular solution of $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}-\\frac{y}{{x}^{2}}=0$ that satisfies the condition $y(1)=e$ is\n\n$$\ny={e}^{2-1/x}\\,.\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186323695 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1346830d-69d5-5e73-b008-864e705c06e1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{e}^{2\\sqrt{x}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186321295}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "1346830d-69d5-5e73-b008-864e705c06e1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{e}^{2\\sqrt{x}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186321295} :answer-field/id #uuid "a4536973-5a71-5025-9fa7-f6117f951929" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186323487}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "79c0a17d-464c-5b60-a5f6-f47a7f26752e" :question/math-academy-id "q-355579" :question/problem "Find the particular solution to the differential equation $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{y}{\\sqrt{x}}$  that satisfies $y(1)={e}^{2}.$\n\n$y=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "We first find the general solution and then apply the given initial condition to find the particular solution.\n\nSeparating variables and integrating both sides with respect to $x,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{y}{\\sqrt{x}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{y}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{x}} \\\\ \\int \\frac{1}{y}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int \\,\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{x}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ \\int \\frac{1}{y}\\,\\text{d}y & =\\int \\,\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{x}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡|y| & =2\\sqrt{x}+C\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nApplying the exponential function to both sides, we find the general solution:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{e}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡|y|} & ={e}^{2\\sqrt{x}+C} \\\\ |y| & ={e}^{2\\sqrt{x}+C}\\, \\\\ |y| & ={e}^{2\\sqrt{x}}\\cdot {e}^{C} \\\\ y & =K{e}^{2\\sqrt{x}}\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $K=\\pm {e}^{C}$ is an arbitrary constant. \n\nWe now apply the condition $y(1)={e}^{2}$ to find $K:$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{e}^{2} & =K{e}^{2\\sqrt{1}} \\\\ {e}^{2} & =K{e}^{2} \\\\ K & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, the particular solution of $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{y}{\\sqrt{x}}$ that satisfies the condition $y(1)={e}^{2}$ is\n\n$$\ny={e}^{2\\sqrt{x}}\\,.\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186332811 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3e4267cc-bd1e-507b-8072-2b2a8c2eca09" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=\\frac{2}{x}-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186330502} {:answer/id #uuid "8b417cb4-a6eb-5024-a1dc-ec0c00436d6b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=\\frac{1}{2x-1}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186331187} {:answer/id #uuid "a75d6c04-3e75-57b3-9835-3be982051f16" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=\\frac{2}{x+1}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186331442} {:answer/id #uuid "bea71f13-c9fb-5f86-9a53-ebbcbb8c38fc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=\\frac{1}{x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186331639} {:answer/id #uuid "ee34aca2-9235-5324-8e51-2b3c711ec180" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=\\frac{1}{2-x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186332039}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "bea71f13-c9fb-5f86-9a53-ebbcbb8c38fc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=\\frac{1}{x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186331639} :answer-field/id #uuid "d34e31ac-faf7-57f2-94a5-145454205303" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186332572}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "35b7f010-fdff-57b3-be05-b9c6b80db56c" :question/math-academy-id "q-50220" :question/problem "Find the particular solution to the differential equation $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+{y}^{2}=0$ that satisfies $y(1)=1.$" :question/worked-solution "We first find the general solution and then apply the given initial condition to find the particular solution.\n\nSeparating variables and integrating both sides with respect to $x,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+{y}^{2} & =0 \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =-{y}^{2} \\\\ -\\frac{1}{{y}^{2}}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =1 \\\\ -\\int \\frac{1}{{y}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}y & =\\int \\,\\text{d}x \\\\ \\frac{1}{y} & =x+C \\\\ y & =\\frac{1}{x+C}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe conclude that the general solution is\n\n$$\ny=\\frac{1}{x+C},\n$$\n\nwhere $C$ is an arbitrary constant.\n\nWe now apply the condition $y(1)=1$ to find $C:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}1 & =\\frac{1}{1+C} \\\\ 1+C & =1 \\\\ C & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, the particular solution of $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+{y}^{2}=0$ that satisfies the condition $y(1)=1$ is\n\n$$\ny=\\frac{1}{x}.\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186332825 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "42390f84-76dd-513e-bfe6-30ef499d0b47" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=\\sqrt{2{x}^{2}-4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186330541} {:answer/id #uuid "551e91b4-3be3-55eb-845f-b95b7bb7e037" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-y=\\sqrt{x+4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186330716} {:answer/id #uuid "625cb73d-fe58-5029-93a5-e925201b1fec" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-y=\\sqrt{2x+7}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186330831} {:answer/id #uuid "a48b421b-d946-5b0e-a7fc-5f7cf21d83e0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=\\frac{1}{4}\\sqrt{2{x}^{2}-2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186331427} {:answer/id #uuid "b2fdf6fa-d413-5479-9d2f-edfcccbd37ff" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=\\sqrt{{x}^{2}-8}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186331529}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "b2fdf6fa-d413-5479-9d2f-edfcccbd37ff" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=\\sqrt{{x}^{2}-8}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186331529} :answer-field/id #uuid "47c02f84-950c-5f01-8fca-af4deaa528ad" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186332319}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "11569a87-05ce-5134-b8db-439ec10012e5" :question/math-academy-id "q-50566" :question/problem "Find the particular solution to the differential equation $y\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=x$  that satisfies  $y(-3)=1.$  Assume that $y>0.$" :question/worked-solution "We first find the general solution and then apply the given initial condition to find the particular solution.\n\nSeparating variables and integrating both sides with respect to $x,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =x \\\\ \\int y\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int x\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ \\int y\\text{d}y & =\\int x\\text{d}x \\\\ \\frac{{y}^{2}}{2} & =\\frac{{x}^{2}}{2}+{C}_{1} \\\\ {y}^{2} & ={x}^{2}+2{C}_{1} \\\\ y & =\\pm \\sqrt{{x}^{2}+2{C}_{1}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAssuming $y>0,$ the general solution is\n\n$$\ny=\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+C},\n$$\n\nwhere $C$ is an arbitrary constant.\n\nWe now apply the condition $y(-3)=1$ to find $C:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}1 & =\\sqrt{(-3)^{2}+C} \\\\ 1 & =\\sqrt{9+C} \\\\ C & =-8\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, the particular solution of $y\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=x$ that satisfies the condition $y(-3)=1$ is\n\n$$\ny=\\sqrt{{x}^{2}-8}.\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186351480 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "30133227-afe7-5481-8b20-5e90c5357811" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y={e}^{-x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186348873} {:answer/id #uuid "8dc6e6b6-7795-585f-872f-9399fa1852a0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186349722} {:answer/id #uuid "9fbd50f1-5563-5199-adb8-f88d72b3446a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y={e}^{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186349870} {:answer/id #uuid "dfced686-1189-5123-b1b4-8db75ac9b85e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y={e}^{x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186350409} {:answer/id #uuid "f802ca15-9874-59ee-8efd-bf70059a7581" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=x+1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186350620}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "dfced686-1189-5123-b1b4-8db75ac9b85e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y={e}^{x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186350409} :answer-field/id #uuid "643eee79-9b2d-5db9-9284-6c2e12ddc115" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186350874}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "4c64ef55-bd01-5eed-b990-f133790399ab" :question/math-academy-id "q-8308" :question/problem "Find the particular solution to the differential equation $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=y$  that satisfies $y(0)=1.$" :question/worked-solution "We first find the general solution and then apply the given initial condition to find the particular solution.\n\nSeparating variables and integrating both sides with respect to $x,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =y \\\\ \\frac{1}{y}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =1 \\\\ \\int \\frac{1}{y}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int \\,\\text{d}x \\\\ \\int \\frac{1}{y}\\,\\text{d}y & =\\int \\,\\text{d}x \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡|y| & =x+C\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nApplying the exponential function to both sides, we find the general solution:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{e}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡|y|} & ={e}^{x+C} \\\\ |y| & ={e}^{x+C}\\, \\\\ y & =\\pm {e}^{x+C} \\\\ y & =K{e}^{x}\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $K=\\pm {e}^{C}$ is an arbitrary constant. \n\nWe now apply the condition $y(0)=1$ to find $K:$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}1 & =K{e}^{0} \\\\ K & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, the particular solution of $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=y$ that satisfies the condition $y(0)=1$ is\n\n$$\ny={e}^{x}\\,.\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186351481 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2fa50117-feef-5fb7-b4c7-8d5074635199" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2y)=1-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186348870} {:answer/id #uuid "452eaa99-839b-51b9-a716-4597a4aef31a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2y)=2(1-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x))" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186349076} {:answer/id #uuid "74fe7c6f-36b4-5a32-8184-47101e4f7568" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2y)=1-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186349521} {:answer/id #uuid "80940eb3-3340-5a50-8f91-c8c22404f7b2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2y)=2(1-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x))" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186349628} {:answer/id #uuid "b1b526ac-6987-52e6-b725-e6fce574b42d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2y)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186350018}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "80940eb3-3340-5a50-8f91-c8c22404f7b2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2y)=2(1-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x))" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186349628} :answer-field/id #uuid "aae6146f-444d-5de3-893c-8276bf7fa71f" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186351001}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "61f94262-435d-5c53-971b-8da63f6d82a0" :question/math-academy-id "q-8309" :question/problem "Find the particular solution to the differential equation ${y}^{′}=\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x)}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2y)}$ that satisfies $y(0)=0.$ Assume that $-\\frac{\\pi }{4}<y<\\frac{\\pi }{4}.$" :question/worked-solution "We first find the general solution and then apply the given initial condition to find a particular solution.\n\nSeparating variables and integrating both sides with respect to $x,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x)}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2y)} \\\\ (\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2y))\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x) \\\\ \\int (\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2y))\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int \\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ \\int \\operatorname{cos}⁡(2y)\\,\\text{d}y & =\\int \\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ \\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2y) & =-\\frac{1}{3}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x)+C \\\\ \\operatorname{sin}⁡(2y) & =-\\frac{2}{3}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x)+{C}_{1}\\,,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere ${C}_{1}=2C.$\n\nWe now apply the condition $y(0)=0$ to find ${C}_{1}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y(0) & =0 \\\\ \\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\cdot 0) & =-\\frac{2}{3}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3\\cdot 0)+{C}_{1} \\\\ \\operatorname{sin}⁡(0) & =-\\frac{2}{3}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(0)+{C}_{1} \\\\ 0 & =-\\frac{2}{3}\\cdot 1+{C}_{1} \\\\ {C}_{1} & =\\frac{2}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo we get the particular solution in implicit form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2y) & =\\frac{2}{3}-\\frac{2}{3}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x) \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{3}(1-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x))\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we can write the above equation as\n\n$$\n3\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2y)=2(1-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x)).\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186371852 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "158f36f0-5c9f-5df4-b307-3df00e1927ac" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=\\frac{2}{\\pi +2-4\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(x)}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186371854} {:answer/id #uuid "394931cf-7701-52c5-939f-4d7925927a5c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=\\frac{4}{\\pi +4-4\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(x)}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186371855} {:answer/id #uuid "8a89762a-446c-538c-a5f1-db6c984332b1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=\\frac{1}{\\pi +1-\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(x)}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186371856} {:answer/id #uuid "20d10a3f-7efd-565e-8b3b-25e4aed18fc9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=\\frac{1}{\\pi -4+4\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(x)}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186371857} {:answer/id #uuid "38fc6ca0-2882-53b5-a94f-8c1db20782e6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=\\frac{4}{\\pi -1-\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(x)}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186371858}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "394931cf-7701-52c5-939f-4d7925927a5c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=\\frac{4}{\\pi +4-4\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(x)}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186371855} :answer-field/id #uuid "0e6da82b-caa9-5a2a-8f75-9c0f26f5be8b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186371853}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "a14aaf63-21a2-55cb-ab96-06f1083b96bc" :question/math-academy-id "q-14309" :question/problem "Find the particular solution to the differential equation $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{{y}^{2}}{{x}^{2}+1}$  that satisfies $y(1)=1.$" :question/worked-solution "We first find the general solution and then apply the given initial condition to find the particular solution.\n\nSeparating variables and integrating both sides with respect to $x,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{{y}^{2}}{{x}^{2}+1} \\\\ \\frac{1}{{y}^{2}}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}+1} \\\\ \\int \\frac{1}{{y}^{2}}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int \\frac{1}{{x}^{2}+1}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ \\int \\frac{1}{{y}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}y & =\\int \\frac{1}{{x}^{2}+1}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ -\\frac{1}{y} & =\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(x)+C \\\\ \\frac{1}{y} & =-(\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(x)+C) \\\\ y & =-\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(x)+C}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe conclude that the general solution is\n\n$$\ny=-\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(x)+C}.\n$$\n\nwhere $C$ is an arbitrary constant.\n\nWe now apply the condition $y(1)=1$ to find $C:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}1 & =-\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(1)+C} \\\\ 1 & =-\\frac{1}{\\frac{\\pi }{4}+C} \\\\ C & =-\\frac{\\pi }{4}-1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, the particular solution of $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{{y}^{2}}{{x}^{2}+1}$ that satisfies the condition $y(1)=1$ is\n\n$$\ny=-\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(x)-\\frac{\\pi }{4}-1}=\\frac{4}{\\pi +4-4\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(x)}.\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186375167 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4da866c3-3d7f-5b0b-a810-ac33ce6a862f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{ln}^{2}⁡y=(1-x)^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186375169} {:answer/id #uuid "ff36e7bd-a5e4-5f39-b596-7ff7d49622b4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{ln}^{2}⁡y=1-2{x}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186375170} {:answer/id #uuid "b064fdd0-a36c-5cee-92e7-9e10247d699e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{ln}^{2}⁡y=1-{x}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186375171} {:answer/id #uuid "7c31dc57-4c05-53a8-8a8e-94c43bdec15e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{ln}^{2}⁡y={x}^{2}+1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186375172} {:answer/id #uuid "86e781a0-f04d-53d1-ab6b-bd9ecd3bb5ae" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{ln}^{2}⁡y=2{x}^{2}+1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186375173}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "b064fdd0-a36c-5cee-92e7-9e10247d699e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{ln}^{2}⁡y=1-{x}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186375171} :answer-field/id #uuid "f8c617fb-7a13-5c0b-b392-e9e8047bec0c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186375168}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "0ff30466-1dca-57e2-9c20-c453c1fbb920" :question/math-academy-id "q-50219" :question/problem "Find the particular solution to the differential equation $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=-\\frac{xy}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡y}$ that satisfies $y(0)=e.$" :question/worked-solution "We first find the general solution and then apply the given initial condition to find the particular solution.\n\nSeparating variables and integrating both sides with respect to $x,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =-\\frac{xy}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡y} \\\\ \\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡y}{y}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =-x \\\\ \\int \\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡y}{y}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}\\,\\text{d}x & =-\\int x\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ \\int \\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡y}{y}\\,\\text{d}y & =-\\int x\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ \\int \\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡y}{y}\\text{d}y & =-\\frac{{x}^{2}}{2}+{C}_{1}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo calculate the integral in the left-hand side of this equality, we use a substitution $u=\\operatorname{ln}⁡y.$ Then,  we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}y}=\\frac{1}{y}\\,⟹\\,\\text{d}u=\\frac{1}{y}\\,\\text{d}y.\n$$\n\nSo we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡y}{y}\\text{d}y & =\\int \\operatorname{ln}⁡y(\\frac{1}{y}\\,\\text{d}y) \\\\  & =\\int u\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\frac{{u}^{2}}{2}+{C}_{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{ln}^{2}⁡y+{C}_{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{2}{ln}^{2}⁡y+{C}_{2} & =-\\frac{{x}^{2}}{2}+{C}_{1} \\\\ {ln}^{2}⁡y & =-{x}^{2}+2({C}_{1}-{C}_{2}) \\\\ {ln}^{2}⁡y & =C-{x}^{2},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $C=2({C}_{1}-{C}_{2})$ is an arbitrary constant.\n\nWe now apply the condition $y(0)=e$ to find $C:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{ln}^{2}⁡e & =C-{0}^{2} \\\\ {1}^{2} & =C \\\\ C & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo we get the particular solution in implicit form:\n\n$$\n{ln}^{2}⁡y=1-{x}^{2}.\n$$"}] :knowledge-point/title "Finding Particular Solutions of Differential Equations by Separating the Variables"}]}
